      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>ALuva" Mel )( )I_l7 AI I PATIMT 514M14.• L A 'SUM Fl JOU TRH NST 11-IE EXAMINE!? FiJI) Y APRIL 25 1!-,02 (ALL Rums RannivED.]</head>
        <p>ALuva" Mel )( )I_l7 AI I PATIMT 514M14.• L A 'SUM Fl JOU TRH NST 11-IE EXAMINE!? FiJI) Y APRIL 25 1!-,02 (ALL Rums RannivED.]</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS.</p>
        <p>7ashions for early slimmer are almost fully declared, and, if anything, are more elaborate deb season than last. There is a considerable amount of amusement to be derived from watching these tentative modes make their debut in the world M dress, for many appear only to disappear at once though others again betray at once to the educate eye that they possess the elements of success, and will endure throughout the season and possibly linger on to another.</p>
        <p>Grass lawn is a fabric which has again becom€ popular, and used as a trimming in conjunction with foulard, the effect is charming. It make its appearance under many guises, is of a delight- fully silky consistency, and does not crush nearly so easily as the old kind. Madame la Mode has, however, only lately made up her mind to smile favourably on grass lawn, as there are not such a great variety of patterns as one would expect to End in a material which promises to be so much in -vogue. A few weeks are sure to produce a greater choice, and anyone who has not found the present patterns quite to their mind would do well to wait a little longer till the season is further advanced. There is indeed but a narrow line which divides some of the varieties of grass lawn from a species of coarse canvas or net, of the same tone of colouring, which is much employed for both gowns and hats. Capelines this season will be made of</p>
        <p>• - 1-i-2. I.T . - ;.i. '''..; . -,•44*-1 agfl :yrix; , 1, ) ~ k , 6 ) ,•,, tt ~ty .1,,:;: ,A •ti. 4 \lp p k.- J., 7i w A i .-. i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>PRETTY BLousn.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY BLousn.</p>
        <p>either grass lawn or this net adorned solely with appliques and scarf of lace, or with green leaves arranged in the form of a careless wreath. The pretty blouse illustrated is made of rather coarse spotted net tucked and trimmed with lace insertion. The colour of the model is a light coffee, and a slip of white satin (or silk to match the net) is made to wear underneath. Every early summer seems to bring with it an increase in the number of pretty blouses, and this season the majority are made to fasten down the back, which is a state of &amp;lairs not always convenient where one is not lucky enough to possess a maid, or fortunate enough to have an obliging sister at hand, and a hurried change of dress is required. Lace plays an important part in these as in all de- partments of dress, and indeed it would not be easy to find a dress which is not ornamented with lace or embroidery in some form. Canvas and voile costumes are much in vogue at present, and these show an infinite amount of strappings and tuckings, while laces, enbroideries and &amp;rode?* anglai4e in linen and taffetas are literally introduced to add to the beauty of the tout emsemble.</p>
        <p>Foulard is a material which is one of the exceptions which go to prove the rule that Fashion is a fickle jade, for the last two seasons it has remained high in popular estima- don, and this season gives no sign of any failure ia this respect, It always has found a place in</p>
        <p>- .v. 90 ..... _ . . : ~....f.A.‘ ..„ ~...... ift, - ~...si. • ~47- // . . . , ~.. ,.. - .., 1 , 4 - ' • 6 Ili IM 111 * .- f . ' 4 - ' IN ' I / • g rill .... /,, ! ; ",, ..,:., , •, ....„,,, . it ~....,„,.,,...._--......„......,_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>FOR AFTERNOON WEAR.</head>
        <p>FOR AFTERNOON WEAR.</p>
        <p>the well.- dressed Parisienne's summer ward- robe, and is the first thing for morning wear while the better qualities, liberally adorned with lace and embroidery, are perfectly suited to many festive Society functions. The elegant toilette depicted is of foulard of a lovely colour called zenith, one of the novelties this year. The colour is a lovely misty greyish blue which har- monizes excellently with the trimming of em- broidered grass lawn. The skirt is one of the slew pelerine variety, the upper one having at the corners an applique of pale pink silk roses, while the bodice has a similar adornment on the collar, the vest also being pink in a hand- some brocaded silk. The hat is of the em- broidered grass lawn and pink roses, with a touch of black velvet ribbon.</p>
        <p>Foulard is so delightfully soft that it lends itself readily to the plissé skirt so much affected by slender women, but to be altogether avoided by those inclined to embonpoint. Very pretty also are the foulards which are shot with different colours, the surface of the material being covered with minute spots in either black or white, arranged in irregular groups. Some, again, show a somewhat indistinct check or tartan effect, while others are patterned with zigzags in the manner we were made familiar with last year. Soft grey foulard with a satiny anrface, or black of the same description liberally trimmed with ecru lace, is exceedingly becoming to a matronly figure.</p>
        <p>The smart little costume sketched is for a young girl about fourteen, and is made of pale heliotrope canvas trimmed with narrow pipings</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>%MAUI' COSTUME.</head>
        <p>%MAUI' COSTUME.</p>
        <p>of velvet and bands of lace insertion. The cape collar is of tucked muslin bordered with lace. This tone of heliotrope and a delicate rAreilit, will be much worn this season, together with thampignon and silver grey, for there is a marked tendency towards the adoption of light colours, Some, indeed, of the dresses to be seen already are of satin-faced cloths or zibelines, so light And delioto ii tone toi to verge on white,</p>
