      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>TIIE FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>TIIE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The supreme and indeed almost the. only topic of interest among Free Churchmen at present is the Education Bill. The speech of</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Balfour in introducing the measure raised expectations which a study of the Bill itself has destroyed. It is now clear that Free Churchmen are determined to offer the Bill the most determined opposition, and that we are on the eve of one of the most exciting and bitter struggles in our religious and political history. Nonconformists are concentrating their opposition to the Bill mainly on one point —namely, that it provides for the support of denominational schools from the rates with- out real or adequate public control, and that in many of these schools the children are taught to despise and hate Nonconformity. "It is in- tolerable," Mr. Price Hughes has said, "that we Free Churchmen should be compelled to pay rates for the maintenance of schools where the children—it may be our own children—are taught to contemn our own doctrines."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>ST. JAMES'S HALL CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>THE ST. JAMES'S HALL CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The National Free Church Council has placed itself at the ;lead of the opposition to the Bill. The Conference at St. James's Hall last week was among the most memorable gather- ings in the history of Nonconformity. There have, of eourse, been many great crowded enthusiastic Free Church gatherings in London, and in all parts of England, but the distinction of the St. James's Hall Conference was not its size (though that :was impressive), nor its fiery enthusiasm (though that was unmistak- able), but its thoroughly :representative cha- racter. There were over one thousand repre- sentatives present, responsible and influential men, bearing the mandates of Free Church Councils in all parts of England and Wales, from Northumberland to Cornwall, from Pembroke to Norfolk. The local Councils are taking up the campaign with perfect unanimity and with resolute enthusiasm. The fire which was kindled at St. James's Hall will spread all over the country, and if the Government persist in their course there will be a mighty, conflagra- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>"NO RITE" AGITATION.</head>
        <p>THE "NO RITE" AGITATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. George White, M.P., a well-known citizen of Norwich, is credited with being the first to suggest that, if the Bill is passed, Free Churchmen should offer a passive resistance to the payment of the new education rate, and that they should rather allow their goods to be seized and sold to meet the new impost. Mr. A. E. Hutton, M.P., has also made a declara - tion to the same effect. The British Weekly has taken up the idea with eagerness, and Dr. Parker and the Rev.; H. Price Hughes, M.A., have written to the Times suggesting the same course. At the St. James's Hall meeting every reference to the refusal of the rate was cheered •</p>
        <p>enthusiastically. While not relaxing their energies in the ordinary channels of opposition, many Free Churchmen think it only right that the Government should know at once the nature and the strength of their opposition, and the methods they are prepared to adopt in their conscientious resistance</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>IMINCIPAL CALIM SCOTT.</head>
        <p>IMINCIPAL CALIM SCOTT.</p>
        <p>As we write, Dr. Scott, the Chairman of the Cougregational Union, is in a very precarious condition. His doctor says he may live for a couple of months, but on the other hand a member of his :family has stated that the end may come at any moment. His grave illness :Lust cast a heavy shadow over the coming meetings o".' the Congregational Union. We believe that Dr. Scott's address, to be delivered from the chair of the Union, has been pre- pared, and will be read by Dr. Goodrich, Dr. Scott's friend and neighbour. Dr. Scott has rarely been heard in the pulpits of the metro- polis, and in the South of England he is known only as a name and a reputation. His first and only pastorate was at New- land Chapel, Lincoln, a charge which was subsequently held by one of his most brilliant students, Rev. J. D. Jones, M.A., 8.D., now of Bournemouth. But for thirty-seven years Dr. Scott has been a tutor at Lancashire College, Manchester. He succeeded the famous Henry Rogers as _Principal of the institution, which has greatly flourished under his care.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>REV. J. R. WOOD.</head>
        <p>REV. J. R. WOOD.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. R. Wood, who now enters on his duties as President of the Baptist Union, has. been twenty-eight years in London as the minister of Upper Holloway Baptist Church. Mr. Wood is a Wiltshire man, and as a boy was apprenticed to a draper in Salisbury. But he soon found his proper sphere as a minister of the Gospel. The chapel seats nearly 1400 people, and is always well filled. The membership is over 900, and as Mr. Wood by no means neglects his pastoral duties, this means that he does not find much time for railway trains. Mr. Wood, who speaks from notes, is a quiet, deep, thoughtful preacher, taking good care to feed his people with substantial, wholesome food. He is beloved of his people, because he loves them. The many organisations of the congregation are always in good condition. Evangelistic and open-air work are carried on vigorously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>FREE CHURCH YEAR BOOK.</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCH YEAR BOOK.</p>
        <p>The Free Church Year Book for 1002, pub- lished by the National Free Church Council and edited by Mr. W. Grinton Bury, M.A. is now ready. It is a volume of nearly 350 large pages, and yet is published, so as to be within the reach of all, at the extraordinary low price of 2s. 6d. net. The first part of the volume contains verbatim accounts of the sermons and papers delivered at the recent Bradford meet- ings. The second part includes a mass of information invaluable to every Free Churchman. The statistics are very noteworthy. From these it appears that the membership of the Free Churches of England and Wales (1,946,959) is just a little smaller than that of the Anglican Church (1,974,629), while the Free Churches have considerably more accommodation for Sunday- school teachers and Sunday scholars. For the entire English - speaking world the Free Churches are much stronger than the Anglican Church and her daughter churches; the Free Church membership being over 18,000,000, and the Anglican membership over 3,000,000. The Year Book ought to have a large sale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>MOST NUTRITIOU.S.</head>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOU.S.</p>
        <p>E;' g .r. %V ,f</p>
        <p>GFiATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>Cw , °,4</p>
        <p>REAKFAST-SUPPER</p>
        <p>NUMBER 6 PARADE,</p>
        <p>"M. E. FRANCIS" (Author of "In a North Country 4-e.)</p>
        <p>PART 1.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Collins stood gazing meditatively at the tea- table, mechanically rubbing her hands. Her prepara- tions were complete; the kettle was boiling, the pile of light cakes was keeping warm in the adjoining kitchen, the toast—fresh toast—made, and just at the right stage of crispness. Generally Mrs. Collins had tea a little later in the day. She was the most honest of women, but she abhorred waste, and when the occupants of the drawing-room or ground floor left tea in their teapots, or toast in their toast-racks, that could manifestly be of no further use to them, it was a sin and a shame, Mrs. Collins considered, to throw good stuff away. So she generally disposed of It herself, with a sense of righteous satisfaction that more than atoned for any tepidity or flabbiness in the articles consumed. But this was only when Bessie was not at home. She was at home to-day, and what was more her young man was coining to tea. Perhaps it would be more appropriate to say her young nobleman, for Mrs. Collins' pretty daugh- ter was engaged to a real live lord. It seemed in- credible. Though Mrs. Collins had talked of no- thing but " his Lordship " for days and days, and had but just now prepared his Lordship's place at table, and dusted his Lordship's chair with her own hands, she could scarcely even yet believe it. It was true that Bessie was an exceptionally pretty girl, and had been educated "much above the common," as Mrs. Collins frequently reflected with pride. She was also clever and adaptable. On leaving school she had qualified herself for the position of secre- tary by mastering the intricacies of shorthand and type-writing, and had been fortunate enough to obtain an engagement with an elderly maiden lady of "high family." Mrs. Collins frequently alluded to her thus : Bessie's connection with the aristo- cracy had been established from the first, it will be seen. Young Lord Martindale. who had fallen a victim to that young person's charms, was Lady Maria Pomfret's nephew. That noblewoman—as Mrs. Collins sometimes called her—was much taken up with charitable work and in her spare moments de- voted herself to literary composition. The pecuniary proceeds of the last named labours she intended when finished to bestow on one of her pet institutions. She had not yet ascertained exactly what these pro- ceeds would amount to as the publishers had shown an unaccountable reluctance to undertake the pro- duction at their own expense. . „</p>
        <p>Naturally with brain' and hand so busy Lady Maria had little time for petty cares and precautions; so long as pretty Bessie Collins typed daily a suffi- cient number of pages from her own shorthand notes it was immaterial in whose company the task was accomplished. Therefore, when Lady Maria was en- gaged in the East End, young Alp.-,y Martindale not infrequently dropped in to see how his aunt's novel 'was getting on, or to take a lesson'in type-writing. Bessie Collins was as sensible and discreet as she was pretty, and first valiantly sought to discourage these visits. But after all what could she do? She could not forbid Lord Martindale to await his aunt's re- turn in the little back room, where she did her work, and she could not prevent his talking to her, and, by and by the things he said made a deeper impression than she knew; and she began to look forward to the hour when the handsome face came peeping with mock timidity round the door. and to await those curious abrupt sentences of his which implied so much more than they actually said.</p>
        <p>One day Lord Martindale ventured to make open love to her, and then Bessie stopped the click-click- ing of her machine, and jumped up, with her beau• tiful brown eyes flashing. " Go out of the room, my lord, if you please," she said, pointing to the door. " I will not be spoken to like that." "I won't go out of the room," returned my lord. "I say, don't be a humbug, Bessie. You needn't pretend to be surprised. You know very well I've been head over ears in love with you all along." " Well," returned Bessie with spirit, " it is time to put a atop to it now. You must not come here again, Lord Martindale—l'll speak to Lady Maria if you. do." "I mean to speak to her myself," retorted Mar- tindale. "Look here, Bessie, have you got any other fellow in your mind ? Because if not, you 3niglit'as well take me. I thought you were rather fond of Inc. And I would make you a very good husband," be added.</p>
        <p>" said Bessie, and sat down blushing to the roots of her hair. "But—but lam no fit match for you," she added faintly after a moment's pause. " Yes, you are," asserted Algy, whose mode of argument was much of the school-boy order. He had not very long left off being a school-boy if truth were told. " You are the best and the most beautiful girl in the world, rand. I won't marry anybody else. 'Why shouldn't Idoas I like? lam my own master you know. Had a long minority, so there's lots of money saved up. I needn't marry an heiress if that's what you're thinking of. It's much the best thing I could do—to marry and settle straight off. I tell you what, Bessie, if you don't have me I'll go to the dogs." Several of his lordship's acquaintances had al- ready predicted that his career would end in the kennels, for, though be had not very long been of -age, he had proved beyond the possibility of doubt that his will was stronger than his head. But Bessie was horrified at the threat: it was quite true she was fond of Lord. Martindale, who, however rash and foolish, was genuinely devoted to her, and had, moreover, though he was far from bril- liant, certain frank and engaging ways which she bad found attractive. How could she condemn him to such a terrible fate, when she could by a few words not only avert it but make herself exceeding- ly happy at the same time? So she accepted the nephew, and tendered her re- sign talon to the aunt. There was a fine hubbub as may be supposed; but what oared the young folks? Bessie went back to Witham, the little seaside town whore her mother lived, and preparations for the wedding were hurried on as much as possible. She had told Lord Martindale all about her mother, and tho lodging-house, and what humble people they were, and what a poor way they lived in; and he had not seemed to mind. Bessie was Bessie, he had and be loved her just as well as though her mother were a duchess.</p>
        <p>They were to be married in a few days now, very quietly of coarse, at the little church where Bessie had been baptised, and then they wee to go away for a long journey on the Continent. Lord Martin- dale was coming on this particular evening to be in- troduced to his future mother-in-law: no wonder Mrs. Collins was elated and excited. However, as she reflected, nowhere—let it be where it might— could his lordship drink a better cup of tea than that which she meant to offer him—three and twopence a pound it cost, and she had set and skimmed the cream herself. The light cakes and toast were, as we have already hinted, at the point of perfection. As for herself, she was conscious that her black silk, reverently produced from the cupboard where it had lain enshrined in silver paper and lavender, was of the very best quality, and that her gold and amethyst broobh was a valuable and remarkable heirloom. It was a very largo brooch to begin with, and the setting was exceedingly massive; the amethysts, moreover, were of a brilliancy of colouring that caught the eye directly. Bessie would be wearing diamonds by and bye, very likely, but Mrs. Collins doubted whether even the Martindale diamonds could look better than her amethyst brooch did, in its way. Her iron grey hair was brushed to the last degree of smoothness, and plaited with almost inconceivable tightness; her shrewd, aharpsfestured face was not only ruddier than usual but endowed with that glossiness which betokened a plentiful application of soap. It was a pleasant face in spite of it all, kindly, honest and in- telligent. The brown eyes, though less large and bright, netrertheless resembled Bessie's ; the com- plexion which had been systematically scrubbed and battered away had once been like Bessie'e too ; the mouth, though marred by the anxious lines and puckers all about it, could smile brightly. It was smiling now, for the door opened and Bessie came in. Head and shoulders taller than her mother, with a figure pliant with youthful vigour and health, a face soft Erul round and pink and white, brown eyes, all alight with joy and anticipation, brown hair with a natural wave, and little golden lights—this was Bossie, and no wonder the mother's face glowed with pride and pleasure as She looked at her ; but she did not say much, it had never been her way to say much; there had always bum so many things to think of and so many things to do, but ever since her cradle Bessie had felt her mother'slove wrapping her round, and had known without the need for words how strong and close it was. • Mrs. Collins drew near now, smoothing into place a fold of the girl's simple muslin dress. "You'll be wearing silk soon," she said, with who knows how much inward exultation. ‘, It's you that ought to be wearing this dress of mine," sho pursued presently, "but you would have me get one though I didn't want to go to so much expense. It'll come in for your wedding, though, so I don't grudge it; and I will say as your wedding dress is as pretty a piece of silk as you could see. His lordship will be giving you plenty more by and bye, but there von!tbe none Prettier."</p>
        <p>**rio, ihrreeh, mother," returned Bessie grate. fully. "It seems a sin to let you spend so much money, but I like to think you've bought me my wedding dress."</p>
        <p>" I couldn't think of letting it be any other way. It was very kind of his lordship to want to give it you, Bessie, and to want to give me a dress too, but I couldn't think of it. Nay, nay, he may dress you when he's wed you, but my gun l must wear her own stuff as long is she is my gun." " I'll always be your girl, mother," said Bessie with tears springing to her eyes. - Mrs. Collin's eyes were momentarily dimmed too, but she went on in the same tone: "And as for me I'll never be beholden to no one, not even his lordship, Bessie. I can keep myself, thank God. I have always said that—' as long as I can keep myself and the child,' I says, when your poor father died. And I did. The Lord was good to me, we used to be a bit pinched to begin wasn't we, Bessie? But we got along some road. And now there's only myself to keep. Not that I'm complainin', for goodness knows it's been a wonder- ful piece of luck for you, Bessie, and sometimes I feel as if I ought to be down on my knees thankin' God from rnornin' till night. Gracious ! There's the bell ! That'll be his lordship. Don't let him in, Bessie, till I bring the cakes from the kitchen. Just pour the water on the tea while I run for them.' In another minute the tea was made, the cakes were on the table, and Mrs. Collins was curtseying to her son-in-law elect. She could not help curt- seying any more than she could help calling him " your lordship " and "my lord," when she spoke to him at all ; but for the first few minutes the poor woman was absolutely incapable of addressing him. She was much vexed at her own stupidity, but con- soled herself with the thought that his lordship would have eyes and ears for no one but Bessie. In this she was mistaken. Lord Martindale was certainly very much taken up with Bessie, but he took note of many other things too. Without being in the least clever he bad a certain sharpness of perception, and was conscious of a disagreeable shock of surprise on this first introduction to Bessie's home and Bessio's mother. It was one thing to listen to the girl's sweet ingenuous prattle about these things, and to respond, with a certain glow of self-laudation, that they were quite immaterial to him ; it was a differ- ent thing to be brought actually into contact with them. To sit down in that shabby back parlour which smelt of all sorts of things, chiefly pickles Lord Martindale thought;—indeed there were pickles in the chiffonnier. To gaze upon this woman in her awful crackling silk dress—and that brooch— Heavens, what a brooch ! To hear her address ob- servations in a kind of creaking whisper to his future wife, and to know that she stood in the closest</p>
        <p>possible relationship to her—it was really, as he said to himself more than once throughout the repast, too ghastly. Bessie herself with her graceful, gentle • manners appeared all the more charming from the contrast, but her surroundings were utterly unfit for her. How thankful he was at the thought that he was 90 soon to take her away from them Meanwhile he treated Mrs. Collins with an easy affable courtesy which by and bye restored her equa- nimity. She began to feel at ease, even to grow jocose, twitting Bessie with her approaching deser- tion of her and pretending to believe that it would be an immeasurably long time before they met again. "I know what young folks are when first they gets wed," she remarked. " Your Lordship is carrying my gun l away—she'll forget her poor mother before she comes back." Lord Martindale inwardly wished she would, but Bessie interposed quickly. "That isn't likely, mother. If we stay too long away I shall rebel "—looking with shy, arch tender- ness at her lover. " And Martin Towers isn't so</p>
        <p>very far away you know—l shall often be able to run over and pay you a visit. You must learn to spare me sometimes," she added, smiling again at the young man. He smiled in return, but rather constrainedly. The words had given him an unpleasant shock. He had not hitherto taken into consideration the fact that Martin Towers was within a two hours' journey of Witham. His wife would probably wish her mother to stay with her frequently ; she would also be per- petually wanting to visit her. He wondered how long it would be before his friends found out the occupation of that estimable woman. " Where is Lady Martindale ?" " Gone to Witham to see her mother." " Oh, it's the sea- side season isn't it ? Perhaps she has gone to help with the washing up !" Then they would giggle and snigger and remark that it could not be very pleasant for Martindale—it certainly was not pleas- ant—in fact the prospect was not to be tolerated. The lodging-house business must be put an end to at any rate ; as for the unfortunate proximity of Bessie's future and former homes it might be re- medied in some way :—Martin Towers could not, of course, be moved without the assistance of a magical lamp, but might not Mrs. Collins be induced to go a little farther off ?</p>
        <p>While vague indistinct suggestions were present- ing themselves to his brain he was conscious with sonie irritation of the close and loving understand- ing between the mother and daughter, the tender glances which they exchanged, and Bessie's evident anxiety that Mrs. Collins should not feel herself left out in the cold.</p>
        <p>"Do you know I am quite jealous of your mother ?" he said half playfully, half resentfully, when they strolled away together after tea. - Bessie's eyes sought his anxiously : "You are only joking, I know," she said with a timid note of appeal in her voice. "You would not wish me to change to my mother because I love you. " You belong to me now," he said. "Perhaps I want all the love." " You should not have any of it if I thought you were in earnest," she retorted, with that quick flash in her eyes which he had seen once or twice before. " I should not love you if I thought you wanted me to forsake my mother." He responded with an eager protest; he never loved Bessie so well as when she showed spirit, but nevertheless he was puzzled and bewildered. He could not and would not give up the girl, but never- theless the position was untenable. The mother, to begin with, was absolutely unpresentable. Though he was young and foolish, Lord Martindale was fas- tidious and proud and realised very keenly the dig- nity of his own position. He had not, he conceived, lowered himself in his selection of a wife—he con- sidered himself so great a personage, in fact, that the honour of an alliance with him was sufficient to counterbalance any deficiencies of birth and position on her part. But the halo of glory which would en- circle his wife would not extend to his wife's rela- tions. There seemed to be a good many of them unfortunately. During the recent repast Mrs. Collins had mentioned "Aunt Jane," and." Cousin Margaret," as well as a certain connection who had formerly it seemed been an admirer of Bessie's. This person's name was Sam. Whenever Lady Martindale visited her mother, all these good folks would come crowding to see her. Perhaps on Bank Holidays they would even run over to the Towers, claiming admittance on the ground of their kinship with Lady Martindale. Hewrithed at the thought. Something must be done, and that speedily.</p>
        <p>Whilst the Earl of Clonmel was driving from Punches - town race rreerirg on Tuesday, to his residence Bishop's Court, county Kiidare, his hots,' notea. Atter tbe animal had gene a short distarce his lordship jumped from the buggy, handie ipped by baying an arm in a sling owing to 4 Ircent accidcrit. Ile tell heavily, sue- t ,ming severe cuts about the head. He WAS removed to his residence in a semi-conscious state</p>
        <p>SINGING IN A DREAM.</p>
        <p>Wonderful Experience of a Singer.</p>
        <p>Extraordinary revelations come from a young vocalist' at Sanley, a Mins Florence Boulton, who, tr.rn the age of ten, tuts been well known in North Staffordshire as a religious public singer, singing at religious concerts and P. S. A. gatherings.</p>
        <p>" Sta-nding be-fore a thousand people," she said, "X never felt the least. uneasiness. I remember on several occasions I went through the performance almost with out knowing what I was doing. It was as though I was singing in a dream."</p>
        <p>"Asked bow e accounted for this, Miss Poulton said : "Three years ago I was engsged on the staff of a public institution in this district, and up to then had enjoyed good health, as I do again now, thanks to Dr. Wil jams' pink pills for pale people. But abut the time mentioned I began to suffer from indigestion and sick- ; fleas, A doctor said. I bad chronic dyspepsia, and eventually I bad to me up my situation. Up to a few months back I stayed at borne suffering from the attack.</p>
        <p>"My illness was of a depressing' character. Pains In the chest asd across my hack, and a mouth and tongue always in a friehtful state repue- names for food and actual sickness made my lift</p>
        <p>A we]</p>
        <p>:norm figure on the concert platform.</p>
        <p>--- perfect misery. Even if I took a cup of water, it would all aroma up. In consequence of my inability to take proper nourishment I wan very weak, with sinking and and fainting feelings attended by palpitation. from which I suffered very much. I often felt I was swoon— ing, aod though I never actually fell, many a time I said, am going to fall.'</p>
        <p>"Some nine months ago," continued Mies Boulton, "Dr Wilhelm' pink pills for psis people were recom- mended to me. I saw an improvement in my condition, after taking the first box. I left off taking them twe months ago, and have bad no occasion to renew them or to take any other medicine since. My digestion is per- fect. Dr Williams' pink pills.forrpalo people did for me what everyttiog else failed to do; I had taken many things" (aid Mies Boulton named half-a-dczen prepara- tions which had failed to give her lastins benefit) ; "while," she added, "Dr Williams' pink pills gave me ease directly. lam now in excellent health, and able to resume my employment. All my friends notice a great Improvement in my appearance and health." Miss Boulton, who lives at 40, Buxton-street, Sneyd Green, Hanley, made the above s atenaent to a repre- sentative of the " Staffordshire Sentinel " The same lack of power to digest and extract nourishment from food which she describes, sfflicts thousands and the sad result is seen in haggard men and women whose lives are embittered by perpetual gnawing pain anal general disturbance. It is madness to attempt to cure such dis- orders by purging. The stomach requires " tone," and the very strength necessary is supplied by Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people, which enrich the blood in a manner unequalled by any other medicine. These pills are w,riel famous through their woneerful success in all disorders arisiog from impovished blood, aneetnia, rickets, scrofula. " decline," consumption. indigestion, palpitatien, rheumatism, sciatica, St Vitus dance, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, neuralgia, and all disturb- ances of the nervous system. The price is two shillings and ninepence for one box,or for six boxes thirteen and nine, sent direct by the manufacturers, Dr Williams' medicine company,llolborn-viaduct, London, or obtain- able of medicine dealers. Unfortunately,intending pur- chasers aresometimes deceived into accepting worthless substitutes, but the propmties of the genuine pills have never been imitated, and customers will find safety by mentioning and noticing the name in full, Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people,on the packet handed to thorn.</p>
