      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Duchess of Marlborough will attend the Queen on her Coronation. The Maids of Honour have, of course, a prcminent place, but it is an old custom that upon much occasions the Queen is attended to by the youngest of the ladies who can claim the title of grace. If this precedent was strictly followed, the honour would fall upon the present Duchess of Manchester, who, like the Duchess of Marlborough, is an American, but she hes not yet been presented to their Majesties the Ring and Queen. The Americans of whom there are several in society circles,are jubilant aver the fact that the honour falls to ene of their countrywomen ,the Duchess of Marl- borough, who is a singularly graceful and dieting uished looking woman. Mrs Sarah A Tooley gives a most interesting and authoritative biography of her Majesty in " The life of Queen Alexandra," published by Meaner Hodder and Stoughton. The writer is the author of" The Personal Life if Queen Victoria," and has been granted special facilities by her Majesty for making her present work as complete and as interesting as possible. It contains many illustrations and deals with the Queen's early life in Denmark, where the order of the day was early hour, and plain living. The only novel which the Queen read before her marriage was "The Heir of Redeliffe," and ne machine was used in the making of her trousseau,which was made almost senirely in this country. This was almost before the days of sewing machines, but there were re:lways, and when the Queen journeyed to London On the occasion of her marriage, she left Copenhagen en an engine named Blucher, with a driver named Welling- ton. Mrs Tooley also gives an account of the Queen'a triumphal entry iota London ass bride,and the number- less public duties she has ever since taken up. Of the Queen's present interests and amusements, we are told that bantam rearing is her latest bobby, and her Majesty's love for dogs and pets is well known. Her favourite studies are music, photography, and water- eclour drawing, and of games, she likes croquet, but does not care for tennis, and only occasionally playa golf. The Queen is also fond of fishing, but does nO" share the King's enthusiasm for the motor-oar. So many contrary assertions have been made) about the Queen's favourite flower, that it is interesting to learn on undoubted authority that the pink malmaison is invari- ably her Majesty's choice for the decoration of her private roc ms. Typhoid seems to have a singular predilection net only for royal personages, but also for the titled and wealthy classes. When King Edward, then Prince of Wales, contracted the disease some thirty years ago, the source of infection was &amp;finitely ascertained, but though there are several theories accounting for Queen Wilhelmina's attack, none of them seem reliable. The Queen, whose life seems to have been full of troubles since she came to the throne, unfortunately took a chill whilst walking in the gardens of Het Loo, and this no doubt made her more susceptible to an attack of fever Still typhoid can only come from direct infection, and it is surprising how many royal personages fall victims to this dread disease seeing the care and precautions that are taken to guard them against such risks. Sydney Smith seems to have comprehended pretty exactly the characteristic virtues of an Irishman, when he deseribed him as " brave, witty, generous, eloquent, hospitable, and open-hearted." A conspicuous example of the generosity of the Lush people has been offered , recently by a subscription for the widow and family of Dr. William Smyth who died from typhoid fever in con- sequence of his heroic efforts to succour the people on a small island off ceunty Donegal, where an outbreak of typtinict had taken place. The total amount subscribed was £7,629,0f which nearly nine-tenths was collected in Ireland. The medical journals are always discovering terrible dangers in every day customs, and, recording to the Lancet, the practice of placing the morning letters on the breakfast table is full of deadly perils. Nine times out of ten, both the stamp and the envelope have been licked by the sender, and the fprocees of opening the letter is likely to release any materies morbi that may be present. Then there are the risks of infection to which a letter is expesed, in transit, and, says the" Lancet,' the post is " probably a very wide distributing agency of all sorts and conditions of organisms" We are there-' fore strongly advised not to have our fritters placed upon the hreakfast table, though probably few will be disturbed in their minds by this freak warning. Similar risks attach to the morning newspapers, and the morn- ing milk can, or even the morning walk, Fleeing that read dirt abounds in microbes, but one had surely better die at once than worry about all the dangers to health which latter day saienee has discovered for us. In view of the near approach of the Cermsation, few designs in red, white, and hue, appear to be finding a place among wearing apparel. At the time of the Jubilee fifteen years ago,end seain five years etee,great facilities were offered UR for presenting ourselves in the National colours, beet the ordinary purchacer proved chary of this privilege, and a coneiderable portion of such strck was left an the manufacturer's and shop- keeper's hands. Among tbe lower classes of women- kind, some little business will he don. There is the round sailor hat with its red, white, and blue band, or the plain dark blue band with the Royal Arms worked or printed on it ; there is also the red, white, and Hue necktie; and the startling blouse which appears in the National colour.. Evert in this " Circle " however, an improved taste in dress has long been marled ; endless conspicuous and dating attire will doubtless be preferred by the majority of working class; women. Sequins are being plentifully employed agaie, the ' mother-of-pearl variety being perhaps the favourite at the moment. Silver sequins, beweerr, an white tulle, or chiffon gowns are esjoying a wide vogue, and on coloured toilettes they are so assorted and shaded as to give a shot effect. Regerding buttcna, the decorative varieties are of greater value tbaa ever before. Rosettes of eilk or velvet bows of narrow velvet featoons of deli- cate drapery, all require the finish of the übiquitous jewel button. This is to be essentially a jewellery year, and the array of smart trifles to be seen in the stop windows is truly bewildering, Veils were little worn, even during the winter menthe, and the bleak wind,' that stayed so late, and new they are rarely seen at all. Oceat'oeally one appears over a small closely fittime toque. but it falls only to the uprise lip, and is tied at the back in a loree bow and ende. Running along the nem r edge of these veils 19 a tiny wreath of flowere, which forms a pretty effect about the brim of a tr gut.. when the veil is draped. Hats are to be wider of brim this summer than has been the case for a long time past, and the wise will not fail to avail themselves of this concession on the part o- Dame Fashion. neneath such shads all eyes look their beet, and trequeetionably they are grateful, as the sun gains power,—for sueh protection. Shot, or merged effects are being as much sought after by milliners, as dressmakere,coesequently headgear shows little decisive eolouring,one tone being subtly merged with another. Such hats and toluesare really economical as they can be worn with several toilettes, some shade in them blending with each.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WARRINGTON SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION. ---- Address by the Rev. Morley Stevenson.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION. ---- Address by the Rev. Morley Stevenson.</p>
        <p>'I here was a large attendance at the quarterly meec- ing of the above association, which was held in Fairfield Schools on Monday evening. The Rector (Rev Canon Willis) presided. The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON. yrincipa I of the Train- ing Collf ge, gave an address which took the form of ait " instruction," the subject being " The Church." Th rev. gentleman asked his audience to look back Over the past 1900 years which had elapsed since the Church was founded. Woat a wonderful phenome- non it was ! For 1900 years a certain society had exis- ted, and did exist, and would exist in the world iu which they lived, a society which had provoked th, most deadly hatred on the part cf some,the most terribl- opposition on the part of athlete, with the most awful persecutione. It had been rent with divisions within and assailed with opposition without, and yet it still existed, strong, influential,powerful. (Applause ) Who founded it ? What was it founded for ? How was it differentiated flees other bodies ? It was founded by their Lord and Saviour. The " Church " was not the name the Lord chose to apply to the body they now called the Church. They name he used—and they wou'd read it over and over again—was the "Kingdom of God," or the " Kingdom of Heaveo." The kingdom was unlike earthly kingdoms. It would not as earthly kingdoms did, live for a little while and then perish and come to nothing but woald last for ever. It was not ; like earthly kingdoms bounded by certain boundaries ; it was co-existent with the whole of this word. Al nations might belong to it without sacrificing the loyalty they owed to their temporal sovereigns, and when the kingdoms of this world would all have passed away and vanished into nothing, then this would be the kingdom of all kingdom and the head would be the King of kings and the Lord of lords. (Applause.) Before Pentecost the Lord had founded the Church,founded the kingdom chosen the apostles. instituted the sacraments. The whole Church was ready, but there was no life in it, but when on the day of Pentecost,the spirit of the living God was cent frem Heaven by the Lord and breathed into the Church it sprang into life. That was the birth- day of the Church of Christ. After referring to the ages of persecution and other periods in the history of the church the speaker said that since the Reformation, the Church bad had to deal with difficultiee. They lived now in the days when every- bedy read, and when people were apt to take their relieieue opinions rot so much from their Bible and Prayer Bcok as from some newspaper. In the birth of the modern newspaper—a comfort and a flessing though it was in many ways—in the modern review and magazines, and all the things they saw every day at the railway stalls,they got a mass of littrature which was written to back up not to find out the truth, to back up a particular party or set, a particular phase of opinion, and the effect was that instead of a steady search after truth and the study of the Bible, or some bet* of divinity, there was a: mixture of all sorts of thiegs. That was one of the dangers of the day, and they could not have these great inventions and great things without danger. There never would be a time, in fact, when the Church would be out of danger. The danger, might shift, but as lam as the great enemy of souls lived and went on stirring up opposition to the Church there would always be danger, and in whatever time they lived they vvr ul d base to face it. A vote of thanks having ben accorded to the speaker, light refreshments wers ierved,and a successful gather- ing was shortly afterwards brought to a close.</p>
      </div>
