      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>1 HE STARS.</head>
        <p>1 HE STARS.</p>
        <p>Day gemtly drew her garments reund, And closed her eves in peace profound ; While Nature, still of mothers best, Receic'd her chi'dren to her breast, That they, will her, in Night might share A solace sure for toil and care.</p>
        <p>And 10, o'er Night's unclouded brow, A thousand stars are shining now— Bright eyes that from their heights sublitne Survey the changing scenes of time ; And through the darkness vigil keep While human eyes are sealed in sleep. 0 wondrous stars 7 when hut a boy, Ye were to me a world of joy— A dreamland where, with eager eyes, 1 saw yoer glories round me nee. And long'd to reach, as boys are prone, More distant skies and stars unknown.</p>
        <p>But new it is enough for me, That in your orbs these eyes can see, The same eternal Band Divine Thst made this mortal frame of mine— The God whose mercy, like Die power, Shines brightest in the darkest hour. Shine on, ye stars ; from moor or hill, I love to gaze upon ye cUll; And in your eyes behold the while The bright and long familiar smile That ever still the same appears, Seen through our joys or through our tears. J. H. W. CLINDINNING.</p>
        <p>The " Times," in its political notrs, says :—Althougb the new silver coins are not yet M circulation in this country they are, apparently obtainable on the Conti- nent, for Mr Biddulpb Martin stated in the lobby of the House of Commons last night that in counting a con- signment of silver recently forwarded from Belgium his bank clerks came) acmes several shillings bearing the portrait of King Zia ard VII. on the obverse, and the lion and Crown on the reverse. Si-rangely enough a communication addressed to the Bank of England simply (belted the reply that these coins bad not yet been put nto circulation.</p>
