      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>CIATION CUP FINAL AT THE CRYSTAL PALACE.</head>
        <p>CIATION CUP FINAL AT THE CRYSTAL PALACE.</p>
        <p>THE MATCH.</p>
        <p>The mighty crowd which gathered at the Crystal Palace on Saturday from north, south, east and west, went away disappointed, and will have to make the journey to Sydenham again this week-end if they want to see the Association Cup won, and the coveted medals presented to the winning team. It is extremely unlikely, however, that anything like the host of seventy thousand odd spectators which witnessed Saturday's match will again assemble to cheer one or other of the teams</p>
        <p>- to victory, for even if the enthusiasm of the very ardent supporters has no limits, the same can hardly be said of their pockets, and not everybody ' can spare the time. Still the match is certain to attract a fairly large assembly, for that is always procurable in London, and it is to be hoped that they will be rewarded with a better display of football than was witnessed on Saturday, when, as all accounts agree, the game, after the first ten minutes was decidedly slow, although the finish was exciting enough. The spectators had' already allotted the Cup to Sheffield United when Wood, the Southampton captain, scored the equalising goal. As a rule we do not expect a really first- class display of scientific football in a final tie, but Saturday's exhibition was uncommonly poor. Neither Sheffield United nor Southampton were seen at anything like their best. Foulke, the sturdy . Sheffield goalkeeper, was undoubtedly the hero of the match, but both the rival captains were prominent at certain stages of the game. Wood, as we have seen, saved the situation for his side, and Needham for the hundredth time proved his value as a general emergency man. With the Glasgow catastrophe fresh in their minds, most elaborate precautions had been taken by the Palace authorities to prevent accident, and in this they were entirely successful. The Coronation season has opened with a very unpleasant social scandal in the petition brought by the Marquess of Downshire against his wife on account of her conduct with Mr. G. S. Laycock. It is needless to go into any details, and suffices to say that the petitioner's</p>
        <p>THE LATE DON FRANI D'ABOI6I</p>
        <p>Si•KING CONSORT OF SPAIN.</p>
        <p>TI DOWNSHIRE DIVORCE: THE PETITIONER AND RESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>evidence in an undefended snit showed that he had ample cause for his complaint, and in less than half -an - hour Sir Francis Jeune gave him his freedom. The Marchioness seems to have lived more or less unhappily with her husband up to the open rupture of this year, a standing subject of grievance being the question of keeping a yacht I Vic Miss Katharine Hare, the daughter of Captain Hare, this lovely Irish- woman has ever since her marriage been one of the acknowledged Society beauties, and her portrait is familiar to all from the illustrated papers, which have reproduced it countless times. The Marquess, whose places are East Hampstead in Berks let since the Sling of the petition, and in Ireland, is a little-known man so far as the generaj public is concerned, as he is neither a leading sportsman or politican. The co- respondent, Captain G. S. Laycock, is an immensely wealthy colliery owner, who distinguished himself very much at the front in the Imperial Yeomanry, gaining the D.S.O. and being mentioned several times in despatches. He is a motoring enthusiast and devoted to hunting, a common taste which led to the meeting of the three principals in this sordid social drama in Leicestershire. There are three little children, the issue of the marriage—a son and heir to the title, and two baby daughters. The recent troubles in Ireland have culminated in a strong step by the Government in the bringing into force of the Crimes Act, and the proclaiming of the United Irish League which has been at the bottnra of the various disturbances 'which have been harassing the Emerald Isle since</p>
        <p>=</p>
        <p>ON THE WAY TO WORTHING:</p>
        <p>THE CRIMItig ACT IN IRELAND: THE SHADED PORTIoNS OP THE MAP ARE THE PROCLAIMED</p>
        <p>- ,. ,)1</p>
        <p>TIIE RIVAL CAPTAINS, THE CUP, THE MEDAL, AND A BIRD'S-EYE VIEW OF</p>
        <p>Lue places scheduled are Cavan, Clare Cork, Cork Boroughs, Leitrim, Mayo, Roscommon, Sligo, Tipperary, Waterford, and Waterford Boroughs. The step has come in for a good deal of general criticism, and is highly unpopular in many quarters, and it is even believed that the question caused a pill in the Cabinet. It is said that Lord Cadogan, Mr. George Wyndham and the Messrs. Balfour were strongly opposed to such , a contentious action, but that the Duke of Devon- shire and the Marquess of Londonderry, both large Irish landlords, insisted. It is feared that it may give rise to more serious trouble in these districts. . but, on the other hand, it may be argued that things are in a highly unsatisfactory state and call for some decisive action.</p>
        <p>Don Francis d'Assisi, ex-King Consort of Spain, whose death has just occurred in Paris, was the husband of Queen Isabella 11, but for several years he had lived entirely apart from her, for the royal couple were entirely incompatible in their tastes. The marriage, as some of us well remember, was one of the "in- famous Spanish marriages" or- ganised by Louis Philippe in 1846, marriages utterly condemned not only by the pro- gressive party in Spain, but by allied and power- ful States. By these marriages a queen and her sister, children both in years and under standing were wedded, the former, Isabella 11., completely against her in- clination, and the latter, the In- fanta Maria</p>
        <p>Louisa, a child</p>
        <p>of fourteen ix direct opposition to the wishes and interests of her country, in order to forward an intrigue between the Queen-mother Christina and the King of the French, having as an ultimate con- summation the union of the two countries under French domination. The marriages took place on the evening of October 10, 1846, not only the IFrench, but the Spanish Prince, meeting with a sullen and angry populace. Don Francis was, however, almost immediately created Captain- General of the Spanish Army, also receiving, by the Queen-Mother's command, the honorary title of "King," with the style of Majesty." The dethroned Monarch and Queen Isabella were ex- pelled from Spain by the revolution of 1868, and have since lived in comparative privacy in Paris. The dynasty was restored in Queen Isabella's son, Alfonso XII., who died before his son, the present King was born. Don Francis was nearly eighty. He was ever feeble and undecided in character, unstable in his political opinions, and the very last man who ought to have been chosen to sit at the right hand of a youthful queen, to assist in the government of an unsettled country and a restless people. As a matter of fact, he at first objected to the marriage, urging his more energetic brother Enrique's ambitions; but his feeble mind was overruled by priests, and he gave way.</p>
        <p>This week the fourth Motor Car Exhibition at the Agricultural llall has been open under the auspices of the Automobile Club, and it has cer- tainly been a very brilliant success, over a thousand cars having been put on view. The feature of the whole show from a national point of view has been the display of British cars, which have by now surpassed even the best foreign ones, despite the legal disabilities under which our manufacturers have been labouring—a point which will be viewed</p>
        <p>Ar INTERESTING RUN IN CONNECTION WITH THE MOTOR EXHIBITION AT THB AGRICULTURAL HALL.</p>
        <p>with great satisfaction by all Englishmen. One of the most gazed at automobiles was his Majesty's new 22 h.p. Daimler car, but unquestionably the feature of the Show were the 10 h.p. Lanchester cars, which were exhibited for the first time and show a great advance not only from the engineering, but also the carriage-building point of view. They are essentially horseless carriages of attractive appearance, one actually possessing a detach- able brougham top for evening work. Great progress has also been made in comfort and reliability, and it is a feather in the cap of the unfairly unappreciated British manufacturer. About a dozen of these cars made a most successful run from the Carlton Hotel after lunch on the first day of the Show to Worthing yid Epsom, Dorking, and Horsham, which was accomplished without any stop, except one .at Horsham for tea, and without accident of any sort except one punctured tyre just before the Sussex watering_ place was reached. The uniformity of this per- formance is a great testimonial to the general nee and efficiency of the automobile, when perfected, ,for long distance pleasure trips and purposes of convenience. This Show marks an enormous advance in automobilism</p>
        <p>.</p>
