      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WARRINGION AND THE LICENSING BILL. 7-- Meeting in Parr Hall.</head>
        <p>WARRINGION AND THE LICENSING BILL. 7-- Meeting in Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>The Government's Licensing Bill was under die cusston at a meeting held in the Parr Hall on Thursday last week. There was a good attendance. Mr BA Naylor presided and there were also present on the platform: HO? Canon Hicks, of Manchester, Rev Robert Veitch, of Liverpool, Mr E Boreland, representing the United Kingdom Alliance, Revs John Gunnell, F E Powell, C Finch, J F Matthews, J S Hughes, Councillor Luke Ellison, Messrs James Dodds, R Wyllie, C E Parker, H Taylor, Sherwood and Mr F Gandy (secretary). The platform also ac- commodated the Band of Hope Union Juvenile Choir who sang a number of choruses urder the conductorship of Mr A Spruce. Mr F Pod more, M.I.U. M.. presided at the organ. The CHAIRMAN, in referring to the presence of children at the meeting said the children certainly needed every encouragement to walk in the paths of temperance. Band of Hope work was a great human uplieter. Ha was very pleased to be present that week at a large meeting in Exeter Hall, London, presided over by the Bishop of London, whom he was glad to tell them was a iteetotaler. (Hear,hear.) The Bi•hop told his hearers that he would never rest content until he had an active tem- perance organisation in every parish of his diocese. Al- ready they had in the diocese of ,London 509 Band of I Hope societies (Hear,hear.) The Chairman proceeded to refer to the fact that the King had lent his influence Ito the total abstinence society in connection with the army. Temperance people had a number of things to congratulate themselves upon. One was the way the Government Bill had been received in the House of Com- mons. (Hear, hear.) The extreme temperance mem- bers had united with the other members in blessing and not cursing the measure. (Hear, hear.) He was pre- pared to go on this principle with regard to temperance " that the smallest contributions are thank- fully received." (Laughter.) There were -ome imp Cant points in the measure brought before the attention of Parliament and one was the supervision of clubs. (Hear, hear.) He believed the two political clubs in Warrington did a little in the way of distributing intoxicants. He would like to see clubs entirely free from drink. (Hear, bear.) It was a great shame that in these enlightened days, there were many people who entered the bogus clubs in the back streets of Loudon and elsewhere and sat drinking until the small hours of the morning. It would be a great advantage when clubs were under better supervision. With reference to drinking among women, he saw a statement that within the last 15 years, the deaths from intemperance among the female population of this country had doubled. No one wanted to take the responsibility for this increase of drunkenness among women. The Bill suggested dealing with grocers' licences. The Grocers' Licensing Bill was brought in by Mr Gladstone with the purest of motives, and was hacked up by one of the best men who ever lived, the Earl of Shaftesbury, but whether it bad been a curse or a blessing was a matter of opinion. Mr E BORELAND proceeded to explain the provisions of the Bill. He pointed out that they would be unfaithful to their principles if they did not do all they could to strengthen the hands of His Majesty's Govern- ment in getting the Bill placed on the statute book at the earliest possible moment. The Bill was a very moderate one but it was well worth getting all the same In the first place it provided that simple drunkenness ic public: places ehould be made an offence against the law. He &amp;really that during the next nine years in the history of Warrington it was just possible there might be one publican fined for making people drunk. Another provision in the Bill was that a drunken person in charge of a child under seven years of age in a public place, should be liable to be arrested in the interests of the child. (Applause.) He was hope- ful that such a meeting as that was would induce the member for the Borough to do for this Bill what he failed to do for the children's bill last year and Rive it his support. (Hear, bear.) There rested upon the people of Watrington a great responsibility. 1 hey could make their member of Parliament do almost any- thing they liked if they threatened him that unless he voted for them they would not vote for him at the next election. (Hear, hear.) The Bill proposed, as Mr Monks had already indicated, that a married men who had got a drunken wife should be able to secure separa- tion from that wife and so he ought to be able. (Hear, hear.) But the Bill also provided that where a woman had got a drunken husband she also should be able to secure mparation from the drunken wastrel, and 80 far as the law will enable her to do it,compel him to pay for her support. (Hear, hear.) The Bill did not propose to abolish grocers licenses but it waeproposed totremovelan anomaly in connection with them. At prosent,magistrates cculd refuse a licence for the sale of beer to a grocer but they could not refuse a license for the sale of spirits and wines. The Bill would give the magistrates the discretionary power to refuse a license if they thought fit. It was a well-known fact that the number of full-licenses had been ening down whilst on the other hand the number of grocer'slieenses had been increasing. With regard t 3 drinking clubs, the object of the Bill was to register all clubs under specified con- ditions and if those conditions were not complied with, the clubs would not be registered and they would not be allowed to sell intoxicating liquor. If they did so without registration every one having an interest in the club would be liable to preeecutioa for selling drink without a license. The provisiens of the Bill were good and be hoped they would be able to get the whole Bill. (Applause). The Rev Canon Him, who had a cordial reception moved the following resolution :—" That this meeting heartily welcomes the instalment of temperance reform embodied in the Liceneing Bill introduced into the House of Commons by the Home Secretary and earnestly hopes that His Majesty's government will press forward this measure so that it may become law, without any weakening of its provisions at the earliest possible date. He said he had very great pleasure in moving the reso- lution for many reasons, one being because he shared with the chairman the reputation of being an extreme temperance reformer. They were all agreed that the Bill was a sound measure. They wanted it very much and would be enormously disappointed if it did not pass, and they were determined that if any- thing happened to mutilate the Bill or to hinder its passing into law, the charge of causing that would net lie at the door of the temperance reformers of the king- dom. There were one or two things in the Bill which he took note of. For instance the measure dealt very largely with the regulation and management of drunk- ards, Well, they needed management and regulation. kLaughter.) But the fact was that drunkards were not manufactured by the temperance party, and therefore the temperance reformers did not feel respon- sible for the drunkards. If anyone was respon- sible for the drunkards it was the liquor power and those who insisted upon there being publichouses and liquor ehop s because they could not do without strong drink. The temperance reformers, although they were heartily glad to get the instalment of reform, thought the Bill did not begin with the question far enough up. It waited until the drunkards were made and then tried to regulate them. whereas their (the temperance reformers') idea was to begin further up, and to-night, they saw, they were beginning with the children. The temperance reformers began in the homes of the people, and they thought the Band of Hope work was one of the most valuable helps VI the Church of Christ, and one of the most beneficent agencies for the happinese of the homes of the people. The trade in strong drink was different from almost any other trade in this country, because it was the sale of a poisonous narcotic—aieohol. What differentiated alco- holic drinks from everything Mae was that when they had taken a little they wanted more. The complaint of the temperance reformers was that very few public- houses in the country could flourish unless a consider- able proportion of their customers ware infected with this hideous and accursed crave, When this crave had got hold of a man or a woman their will power declined, the tome power was disorganised, the very organs of digestion were poisoned, and the poor human creature became the slave of one of the most terrible tyrannies they could conceive. The speaker went on to point out that about 70 or N and in some districts 90 per cent of the eases of what were called cruelty, which came under the observation of the N.S.P.C.C. were merely cases of shameless neglect due to intoxicating liquor, and they laid the burden of this hideous cruelty •to chil- dren at the door of the brewers and distillers. They (the reformers; wanted to protect the lives of the children by training them in the principles of total abstiuence in their early years, and fortify- ing them against the great danger. Proceeding, the speaker said the independent Order of ' simile</p>
        <p>ITCHINC NU 'YOURS Rashes, and irritations Instantly relieved and speedily cured by hot baths with etrricunA Soar, gentle applications of CUTICURA Ointment, awl mild doses of CuMcnnA RESOLVENT.</p>
