        <p>OEM</p>
        <p>orders, bad proved to demonstration that it was a healthier thing for a man to be a teetotaler than to be a moderate drinker. Unless the figures which were accumulating on all hands were wrong, they proved that if any young men or young women under twenty would come and join the Band of Hope and stick to it all their life, they would have a reesonable prospect of enjoying between five aud ten more years of active, healthy life, than if they were even moderate driekers. If tney Moat ed God's laws of health and avoided this narcotic stimulant, God re- warded them for obeying His laws by length of life, increase of happiness, and enjoyment of capacity of body and mind. lie could not ' understand why doctors had not been up in arms against drink, long ago, but even they had to learn some things and the lesson of drink and total abstinence was only learnt by long experience. Long experience was now beginning to teach them to open their eyes and to receive the truth. Last July an important congress was held in London on the subject of consumption and tuberculosis. It was convened by tbe sing and embraced as delegates the moat eistingushed and able medical men and scientists trom every part of Europe. ' At that congress a doctor who was at the very head of the medical faculty in France, said " We ca' stamp out consumption and not ouly can we, but we museand we will, but," be said " one of the greatest c mtributory causes to consumption is strong drink and every public) houses is a purveyor of consumption." What an awful indictment of tee drink trade ! Everything they could do towards lessening the number at puteic houses was an effectual step tower&amp; the stampitie out of oonsump- tiou. The latest book on nervous diseases which had , been written by two leading French doctors stated that one of the most potent ctuses of nervous diseases was alcohol. The writers of the b iok said they woudered why their brethren of the faculty did not take up the crusade, for the temperance societies could only deal with the comparatively sober part of the population, whereas the doctor went into every family arid they took his word:for what he told them. The duty ,they said, of every doctor who knew his business, was to warn people against the hideous danger which came from the taking of strong drink The temperance workers had worked for years pat, through good and evil report and bad borne the burden and heat of the day, and now, they seemedeike Eej .I),tosee a cloud like that of a man's hand rising out of tise sea. They saw it in Der:Ritebie's Bill, they saw it in the increash g interest in temper- ance reform in all classes of the community, and they also saw it in the e ttitude of the most advanced medical scientists towards the question. When they saw these signs they ttsanked God and took courage, because they knew that ere long they would convert all thoughtful opineea in the country to their side, and they would see Bills brought before Parliament of a much more drastic kind than the present one, to satisfy the advancing aeiti inereesing convictions of the people. One thing that he wanted to see was a Bill to give the power to every locality, whether in town cr country, like that enjoyed throughout America, Canada, and the British Colenies, of removing the common sale of drink from the neighbourhood if the great majority of the people wanted to be without wnat they regarded as a wrong, a mischief and a snare. He was grateful to Me Ritchie, and wished him more power to his elbow in pushing the BM through the House at Commons. If he found any difficulty it would notcome from them. They wished bim well. (Applause). The Rev ROBERT VEITOEI, M.A.. of Liverpoolmeconded the resolution Re thou,ge they would all rsceenise that there was room for the measure. At the present time the country was spending, roundly, the suns of £160,000,000 per year in strong drink, wnice was equal to an expenditure of Z. 3 pr man, woman and child. They had largely extended the army of teetotalers, but drinking was vastly on the increase amongst those wile did drink, and he thought there was very littl- ques- tioning that there had been a considerable increase in drinking arnengit w m. He thought there was some great elope in ties iestalment of temperance reform contained in the Bill. E aglieti men were excep- tionally tolerant, and very very tolerant of wrong. They stood b a very !erste extent upon the freedom of a men, even though that freedom was very largely the freedom to do wrong. He thought now, however, that the Eeglishman'a conviction was beginning to come I home to him and he was realizing tnat in the matter of national prosperity and national competition, the drink evil was one great item of in- jury that had to be grappled with and restrained. He believed the passing of the Children's Bill was one of the greatest achievements or temperance sentiment that they had witnessed. Tnee had in the present Bill, small though it was—and many of them would vastly extend its previsions—a great promise of hopefulness. They 84w in it sone sort of endeavour to meet the very sad eodditions under which we found ourselves in refer- ence to intoxicating liquors in this country. Continu- ing the speiker said he wished the Established Church and the Free Churches were at one in demanding the passing of the Bill. He regard.d the Bill as a sign of hopefulness Lamm. both political parties were uaited in seeking that its provisions sh mid be put upon the statute book. No doubt the Bill would be very severely handled in committee but he did not fear that the pro- visions were to be substantially carried in the form in which they now fount them. Ploy were very glad to have the opportunity of supporting the measure because it gave the lie to the charge thtt was made against the extremists, that they were never ready to take the half loaf, and that they preferred to go without any broad at all rather than condescend to accept half a loaf .Previous measures had not received their support because, while seeming to ha a reform on the one sid,, they were really inflicting the evil more thoroughly upon them than ever With regard to the present Bill, he held the. when the Englishman saw how much good it would bring, he would be willing to go some steps further. If there wore any persoes pre- sent:who believed the measure would be a solution of the temperance question, let them support it, but all of them could support the mevure as at least being one step in that direction. (Applause.) The resolutim was unanimously adopted and it Was decided to forward cooiee to Lord Salisbury, Mr Ritchie, Mr Balfour, Colonel Pilkington, M P, arid Mr R Pierpoint MP, and also to send it in the form of a patitioa to the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>The meeting concluded with the singing, of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>Rechabitea. tha Sons a Temperance. and .imiTar • tit,</p>
