      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>AWFUL DANGER</head>
        <p>THE AWFUL DANGER</p>
        <p>OF PURGING MEDICINES</p>
        <p>when Consumption threatens or has set in is illustrated by the above cases. What Consumption needs is New Blood, New Strength. Opening Medicines drain the strength Positive evidence exists that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People make new blood and have cured Consumption and "Decline," General Debility, Neuralgia, Eczema, Skin Disorders, Anemia, Indigestion, Rheumatism, Sciatica, Kidney Diseases. After-Effects of Influenza. Rickets, St. Vitus' Dance, Loss of Vital Forces, Paralysis, Spinal Disease, Locomotor Ataxy. Palpitation, and Disorders of Women. If you have any difficulty in obtaining them, or if you are pestered to buy substitutes, you can have a box of the genuine pills direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, _Holborn Viaduct, London, post free, for 2s. 94., or six boxes for 13s. 9d.</p>
        <p>imagined and to give it up would not be the loss they feared. Another reason for discouraging Sunday trad • lag—and he thought a vary cogent reason, was that it was unfair to those who did not and would not opea on Sunday. It did not follow, of course, that all peop'e who closed their shops on Sunday did it on religious grounds. Many of them did it for simply physical reasons, for the sake of rest and relief from toil and business. But whether they closed f rom secular or religious reasons,it was not fair to them that others should open and so divert from them custom which, if they were not shut would probably be theirs. Sunday trad- ing would die out at once if all tradespeople would be loyal to one another. Just as the weekly half holiday would ba possible to-marrow but for selfish people woo would keep open when others, and the majority, wanted to close. Els did not want to advocate boycotting, but it could not be very suprisag if the tradesmen wee kept a whole trade in a town out of a holiday av refus- ine to join in it should be loft s verely alone by cisc- tomr. The selfish conduct of the dew had often been deemed unfair in the matter of the half holiday. The game unfairness existed in the conduct of the few who would keep open on Sunday while others were closed. There eras, however, yet oess other reason wiry Sunday trading Phould be diecountenteneed. It offered considerable temptation to children and yeuag persons to forget the true character of the day of rest. It went a long way to obliterCe the distinction between Sunday arid we,.lc day. Thera were many, doubtless, who had ridiculed and reviled the Elglisn Sunday becaese it had been made so severely distinct and separate from other days. They were doing all they could to remove the distinction. They would have their work ceased, thee mestere' establishments close d, their recreation and rest assured and increased on Sunday, while they would op'n the museums and picture galleries, the music halls, and theatres, the football and crick.t ftelds, and thereby force into Sunday trading and Sunday labour all ' ouch whose services would be necessary to furnish their pleasure. Did it ant, however. increasinaly become Christian duty to pre•erve the rest of the weekly Sabbath, to deliver it from all encroachments, to dis- courage every practice that could increase labour or necessitate trading, as keine inimical to the general welfare—id verse to both phy- siral end. spirituel good. In ainclusion, the rev gentleman appealed to his hearers to help to generate a strong public opinion against all forms of needless trad- ing, and needless labour on the weekly day of rest. It might not be possible to secure the cessation of all kinds of work or even business during all the hours of Sunday, or during all the Sundays for every individual in every walk of life. The needs of modern life had modified the literal application of the verbal command. But, subject to this, the need of the rest day was still existent, even in deeper degree than ever the rieht to it was inherent in every human life, and the Christian aim should therefore be to do everyihing possible to secure it, and to do nothing that could be avoided to hinder its enjoyment by all.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Congregational Church, Newton. Toe annivers fry services in connection with the Sunday School attached to the above church were held on Sunday last, but unfortunately the weather sadly interfered with the conveliene of those attending the services. Notwithstanding the unpropitious state of the weather, there was a large c,ngr-gation at each of the services, especially in the evening, when the Church was crowded. The preacher was the Rev H C Wallace, of Bristol, who last year conducted a very successful mama in Earlestown, and there is no doubt the fame he then established acted as an additional attraction on the present occasion. Those who braved the weather on Sunday morning and evening were well rewarded for their courage, for two more powerful and instructive, and withal appoaliug sormoos it would be difficult to imagine. Tne services otherwise were bright and crisp, and this singing of the choir was all that could be desired Especially may this be said of the singing of the cbil— dren woo had been trained by Me D R Owen, the organist. Toe anthems rendered by the choir were efficiently given and the organist showed a grasp of his duty. In the afternoon a musical service entitled " Coder the Palms" was given by the choir and children a feature in this being the singing of a so'o by a little dot of six years named Elsie Owen. The principal soloists among the noir wc&lt;re Messrs John Svhton, J I Waite, 0 Pritchard, Miss Eckersley (Warrington), Mrs Turner, Mrs Glover, Miss Hughes and Miss „I Howard. Tns discours of the Rev Mr Wallace at night was directed ch,efiv to young men and contained advice of the most excsllent quality. roe collections throughout the day, amounted to about £42, and were in aid of 1. Sunday school funds.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>DEAFNESS AND DISCHARGE FROM</head>
        <p>DEAFNESS AND DISCHARGE FROM</p>
        <p>THE EARS.</p>
        <p>Tnere is • wide-spread belief that Deafness caused by abaueeses in the ears, with injury to the drum-head, is inourable,but the continued suoaess of the Droust treat- ment is a proof that this opinion is wrong. In fact such an unusal nh‘r of cases are reported in the March " Supplement" to the " Journal for the Deaf," that those who suffer from defective hearing, caused by this form of ear disease, will find this publication of great interest. The case of Mrs C Heels, aged 50 years, is a special proof of the succete of the Drones method, as she was cured completely of ' deafuess caused by abecesses of the ears,after treatment at a special hospital , had failed. Her letter of thanks shows the successful result 6, Montgemery-terraoe-road. Sheffield, February 7th, 1902. Dear Sir,—Kindly ac-ept my sinners thanks for bene- fits received under your treatment I feel thst aa expression of gratitude is due to you from me for the restoration of hearing, which has been effected by your excellent treatment.—Youre faithfully. C HOOLE. Those who are in deafness and diseaeos of the ear, nose and threat can obtain a free copy of the " Journal for the Deaf," with the " SuPpierdente" ice addressing the Secretary, Drone Institute, Regent's Park-road, London, N.W. Each " Journal " is accompanied by a Patient's Report Form 'Web enshlee applicants to receive full particulars for appleing th.e treatment at home. Any advice by correspondence II abeelutely free.</p>
        <p>The Manx Tobacconists' Association has instructed an advocate to draw up i petition to the King asking him to withhold his assent to the Juvenile Smoking Bill re- cently passed by both branches of the Manx, Legislature. As the tobacconists did net avail them,' Ives of the opportunity to oppose the measure while it was before the louse, it is not at all likely that their request will be acceded to. - .</p>
        <p>Deporable results have attended the ransacking of the ancient tombs by the Fal!sheen of the Ben Jibrin dis- trict of Palestine. Gold objects have been looted from the grave, which have bees archreologically ruined. Ministerialists (a lobby correspondent states) are in- clined to think that Sir eil Hicks Beach will modify his Budget proposal by fixing £2 as the limit below which cheques shall not be reqaired to bear the extra penny stamp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>CoNsznyerivs MEETING. —The annual meeting of the Warrington Polling District (comprising the townships of Poulton-with•Fearnboad, Warrington and Wooiston- with-Marzinscroft), of the Newton Division Conservative Association was held in the schoolroom, Padgate, on Monday evening. Alderman Prancomb presided.—The Chairman said they had had for a long time a strong Conservative Government in power, and itnaad given, he believed, every satisfaction to the country. It had Oertainly carried on th 2, affairs of the nation with very great success, though it had had a most difficult task to perform. He only hoped that the pro.pects of peace which looked so prominent would result favourably, and that we would soon have an end of this costly and dreadful war. He trusted that when the Coronation came round we would be at peace with the world once more. (Applause.)— On the motion of Mr Mather, seconded by Mr Joseph Stringer, Alderman Frincomb was re-elected chairman. Alderman Sutton was re-appointed hon. secretary, and treasurer, and Messrs W Houghton, A Bennett, and W m Friar, delegates to the Council.—Councillor Bennett moved " That this meeting desires to express its strong approval of the general policy of his Majesty's Ministers and ban every confidence in tbeir efforts to bring the war to a successful termination, and that a prosperous and contented colonial federation will be established in South Africa, and that in due course, Lord Salisbury and his colleagues, will be enabled to carry out beneficial legislation for all classes of his Majesty's subjects, at borne and abroad." He said he was not a thick and thin suppor- ter of the Government, but as far as the resolu- tion went he Concurred very strongly with the general trend of the Government's policy. There were differPnce3 of detail as regarded the Policy of the war and the early stages of the negotiations but they were all of one mind that when the war was begun there was nothing for us to do but to fight it out to the end. With regard to the prospects of peace he hoped we would not attempt to patch up peace by any con- cession detrimental to the empire. He bad every con- fidence that the Government would bring matters to a satisfactory issue. After referring with pleasure to the prospect of a Federated South Africa, he said that general legislation load beers arretted by the war, but when it was over we might look forward to beneficial legislation for all classea. Alluding to the Budget, he said he was glad Ora a tax had bi-en put upon corn and flour. It seemed to him a right way to raise money to tax goods we could produce at home. One result to! this tax was that it would do something to restore agricul- ture to prosperity and give the farmer and that huge mass of population dependent upon agriculture a chance —The resolution was unanimousl7. passed,and a</p>
        <p>vote of thanks haviag been &amp;worded to the Chairman the meeting terminated.</p>
