      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SUNDAY TRADING AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY TRADING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Reference by the Rev. John Yonge.</p>
        <p>At Wycliffe Congregational Church on Bunday evening, the Rev. John longs referred specially to Sunday Trading. He based his discourse on the i text n Nenemiah xiii c., 16 and 17 verses. In refer- ring to the institution of the Sabbath, he said it was a provision for the physical and mental, as well as the spiritual welfare of men. It was the ordination of the Creator who was faithful to His knowledge of the nature and conditions of Ills creatures. The Sabbath was to give man oppor- tunity for physical and mental recuperation and renewal, so that he should do life's work better, fight its battle more successfully, achieve its pur- pose more fully. After referring to the testimonies of Alfred the Great, the late President Carnet and hr Gladstone on the value of a day of rest one day in seven, the preacher seggested that if any were yielding to the present day temptation of allowing Sunday hours to be invaded by week-day work and worry—writing business letters, entering up busi- ness books, studying business plans—they had better re-consider their conduct. And further, it any were milting it indispensable that other people should be constantly deprived of their weekly day of Nit in order to serve their interests, they should consider that also. He was by no means unconscious of the multitude of practical diffioulties with which modern life, and presentday conditions, surrounded the preserv- ation of Sunday from &amp;ocular uses. Viewed from the standpoint of religion, the spreading secularisation of Sunday in thin country was a fact f till of the most sinister portent. It might will make Christian people deeply concerned and anxious, end should certainly lead thorn to be cautious and careful in their own observance of the Lord's day. Christian parents should exercise more kind but firm authority over their young people than was sometimes the cue. He was no believer in measure.' of ceercien awl specially in religioo but he thought that a wise and Christian parenthood would make it *hearty understood that it was expected of sons or daughters, so long as they revaahaed nailer the rule of the home, that they should attead church ou Sunday whenever possible If parents themselves were loyal in their own Sunday keeping, the seas and daughters would be less likely to prove disloyal. He wanted specially to sPeak of one particular form of Sunday misuse ; ho re- ferred to the spreading evil of Sunday trading. Accord- ing to credible testimony this was spreading very considerable in Warrington. Attentim had been specially called to it of late by the Warrington Free Church Council, and that body had issued an address to the miniatere and members,the superintendents and teachers of Nonconformist churches and schools. The first paint in the statement, was that although the evil of Sunday trading existed and was increasing, it was not deemed desirable to combat it by the power of the law. There was a law, of the reign of Charles 11. which made the sale of merchandise on Sunday punishable be' a fine of 5s and by the danger of all geed, exposed far elle being seized by the authorities and ceafisosted. In some parts of the country this old statute was being enforced cow in the way Of proileaution and fine. At Darlington this had been done. At Hull in 1899, no fewer than 4051 persons were summoned, and all except three were convicted and lined. At Exeter, Yarmouth' Bradford and Swansea a similar course bad been followed. It was questionable, heaven', whether this was the best •nd wisest way of dealing with the evil. It was very much better for religious people to employ moral suesion than leeal compuisien. For Christian people to punish be penalty or fine those who differed from their idea of Suuday wou d eat win over the offenders to the better side. It was suggested by the Fron Church Council that leaders of churches and schools and Christian people generally should do all In their power te curtail this growing evil, and on two grounds. One was that Sunday trading was absolutely unnecessary. He ventured to affirm that that was strictly true. There was, there could be, no need what- ever for tobacco shops,towspaper shope,ehops for feed or sweets and fruit, photngraphio studios, to open their doere on Sunday. All those thinee were obtainable until late on Saturday and early on Monday—most ef them could he done without if sufficient forethought had been exercised to procure them previously. There was fin need, sither,for barbers Shope to be open on Sunday. If then all this trading was unarceasery, it aheuld be die- couraged. Buyers should not ask it. Sellers should not grant it. He did not want to say any hard word or to advocate any harsh conduct. But he was confident that for the multitede of little shops in our thoroueh- fazes aad side streets that were open, or that carried on business on Sundays, there was no necessity whatever. Perhaps the shopkeeper would reply : "If I don't sell to my customer en Sundays w'nen he wants me, be won't buy of me on week-clays." 'That was what many keepers of dreg little places of business said in defence sae explanation of their Suaday trading. It &amp;landed like a serious difficulty. But was it as earious as it seemed. Were customers as unreasonable al suggested? Ile did not think so. There had been even some rThriati an people who had thought themselves compelled to sell on Sunday lest they should lose in the weer. But he believed that the facto a toe vnlic and in the len: run were againet them. 0n purel7 Misiness grounds, he believed, Sunday trading was not the gain some</p>
      </div>
