      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>EXA MINE h.</head>
        <p>THE EXA MINE h.</p>
        <p>APRIL 25th 1902.</p>
        <p>Those who know the .IVIr. Chamberlain and . the Education history of the Education Bill, Bill and the controversy Which it caused among Ministers are best'able to appreciate trie courage with which Mc CHAMBER- LAIN defends the measure. Eautizee toujours Zaudgce seems to be his motto, and a measure Which even a progressive Conservative like Sr JOHN GOR3T is compelled to be silent about appears,frona the letter just written to Dr GLOVER to excite the COLONIAL SECRETARY'S fervent admir- ation. Mr CHAMBERLAIN claims the bill as realising one of the :nein objects of the National Education League of 1870, because that organisa- tion desired to see the establishment in every district of a loc-al authority,and expressed a prefer- ence for Town Councils over bodies elected ad hoc. That is not quite the matter to which Mr CHAMBERLAIN principally directed his energies in 1870. At that time he said: " Toe history of this country bears one unbroken testimony to the evils caused by the attempt to maintain any kind of priestly supremacy within its borders" ; and he went on to declare "It is true now, as it was nearly two centuries ago, that Of all the plagues with which mankind is cursed Ecclesiastic tyraany'A the wust." Again, as the "British Weekly" reminds us in an admirable article this week, Mr CHAMBERLAIN fully endorsed the opinion of the Tribune of the people, JOHN BELICHT : "N )thing tends more to Impede the progress of liberty, nothing is more fatal to independence of spirit in the public, than to add to the powers of the priesthood in the matter of education." OE course, we are aware how Air. CHAMBERLAIN meets the charge of inconsist- ency. The bill," he says, "involves no revival of sectarianism, but only the continuance of a prac- tice which has always prevailed, but which is now to be moderated." One might imagine from sueh an explanation that the bill was a cunning design to restrict ecclesiastical influence. What are the facts? The Imperial Government proposes to take over the pecuniary responsibility for all the itroluntary Schools existing, provided that the buildings in which they are held are kept up. If this shifting of burden is not adding to the power of sectarianism it would be interesting to learn What steps could be effectually adopted to that end. Lot us take the analogy of a theatre whose actor-manager is in straitened financial circum- stances. Let it be said that the State, for the pur- Pose of an experiment, would say to that gentle- Man "We are pleased with your performances, and will pay all your expenses if you will keep on in the same building and maintain it in repair. We</p>
        <p>hall nominate one-third of the Bard of image- ment ; but, of course, they can be out voted, and We shall retain the power of vetoing appointments</p>
        <p>in your company if they are notoriously bad actors; Otherwise we leave everything to you." Would that be called a moderating influence on the stage P No one can pretend that it would. The Church majority of marager s are to have the choice of teachers, and to draw support from the Noncon- formists to enable them to teach sectarianism within their walls. Moreover, the local authority is:not to meet a deficiency of accommodation until it is seen whether the Voluntary School managers can meet the gap. The measure is conclusively one to give rate aid to sectarian schools, which otherwise would have gradually fallen away from efficiency. It has been introduced admittedly to meet a serious situation—a situation created the fact that dogmatic religious teaching was losing its hold on the community and those con- 4rned, for it felt that now or never they must atrike for endowment.</p>
        <p>Munn has been said coffering the Budget. about the decay of the</p>
        <p>rights of private mem- bers in the House of Commons, and the tendency Of Parliament to become an automatic machine lot; registering the decisions of the Government. There is abundant evidence that, although the °toy 3rnment may wish this to be so, such a dtate Of things can never come to pass. Human limita- tions forbid it. The present CHANCELLOR of the -C'XCHEQUER is generally regarded as one of the etrongest members of the Cabinet. He was com- pelled, nevertheless, on Tuesday night to acknow- ledge that, with all the permanent officials at his 1,. hac.,) he has a lot to learn from the House of "'°roinons. The pressure of criticism obliged him t 0 reduce the tax on offal. The official minds at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>Whitehall, notwithstanding their experience of the incidence of taxation, had not realised the havoc which the proposed new tax would produce in the agricultural industry. It it a curious question whence Ministers derive their information when they are preparing schemes for submission of Parliament. The need for secrecy may be the cause of the blunders of succes- sive CHANCELLORS of the EXCHEQUER, but there should be honest men in every trade, in whom even a CHANCELLOR should be able to confide. This is not Sir MICHAEL'S first embarrassment in connection with the Budget. When Mr LOUGH inquired, with perfect propriety, whether various articles containing the produce of grain were to Come within the scope of the duty, Sir MICHAEL said he thought they did, but qualified this by say- ing that he could not speak quite definitely off hand. It would have been thought that if his ob- ject was to raise a certain sum of revenue which he had to name to the House he would have had the sources of that revenue clearly estimated in a table before him. The pity of all this is that pro- posals of a most disturbing character are projected which would not have been framed if they were precisely appreciated. On Tuesday evening Sir MICHAEL had to abandon 21. a cwt. of the duty which he expected to realise on offal. The modi- fication was made because he had no idea in the first instance of the mischief it was likely to do. Pnebably, when he committed himseV to the registration duty on corn, the CHANCELLOR: did not realise that he was going to stand between the children and the food which poverty-stricken parents buy for them. Sir HENRY FOWLER'S in' dictment of the departure from Free Trade was a singularly powerful one. Th,‘re is really no answer to the assertion that the burden will be felt most keenly by the poorest of the poor. The rich eat very little bread, and it forms no appreci- able item in their weekly budget. It is the very poor who suffer. The investigations of Mr BOOTH and Mr ROWNTREE have shown that all over the country some 27 per cent, or even 30 per cont. ot the population are living in want—that is to say, have never enough food to maintain bare physical efficiency. To them the tax simply means a few turns more of the screw of poverty and privation. They are a little poorer, a little hungrier, a little weaker than before. At the same time, the families on the edge of the starvation line are forced across it. That is what the tar mean,' to the poor.</p>
        <p>Many people win be eterested in the result of an action now before the courts, which will deter- mine whether the amateur photographer can be called upon to pay an extra charge for the water he uses in the pursuit of his hobby. There is no question about the professional man; he, like those engaged in any other industry for the practice of which considerable quantities of water are required,receives due attention from the inspectors of the corporations or companies responsible for supply. The matter which the courts have to decide is whether the use by the ordinary amateur of water wherewith to wash his plates is or is not a domestic use in the legal sense of the term. Of course nearly everyone has his camera nowadays, and in many houses there are three or four, and a special water-rate for snap-shotters might be made by a little judicious inquisitiveness to bring in a good deal more than the extra charges for garden- watering do at present. Happily the law seems to take the view that the question can be made one of degree, and the case in which the point particu- larly arises has been sent back to the magistrates for them to take evidence as to the amount of water used for photographic purposes by the defendant in this one action. De minimis non curat lex ; and if it is only a matter of a few pints or quarts every now and then nothing more will probably be heard of it. Most reasonable people, however, will agree that if, as was hinted by the experts, it is tho custom of amateur photographers to leave their plates all night under a running tap, there is a possibility of a waste of water for which the rest of the community should not be called upon to pay.</p>
        <p>An analysis of the figures for the King's Coro- nation Dinner brings out an unpleasant fact regarding the City of London. It has been ' thought necessary, in justice to the area described as the richest square mile in London, to allot tickets at the rate of one to every twenty-seven of the population. This, of course, is exclusive of the inmates of workhouse and infirmary. There is something very eerie about the City of London by night—the huge buildings looking desolate and untenanted, while the traffic is limited to a few heavy vehicles going through, generally westward, from the country, and the red vans bearing the Royal initials "ER." The :footfarers are the policemen, the speci,pl night-watchmen the apprentices who live at the wholesale houses —a custom now declining—" housekeepers "and their wives, and the very poor, who come out, like bats, after dark. These people seem to belong to the shadows. Some of them are sandwich men others are street pedlars, and others are casual hangers-on to tho back street shops—going errands and picking up coppers by odd jabs. When the value of sites in the city is remembered, it must astonish people that dilapidated and insignificant dwellings should still remain in some parts of the area. But for that fact they would have to migrate, and it would be much better for the City as well as for themselves if they did.</p>
        <p>, The temperance meeting at Warrington last Thursday evening was of such a character as to give great encouragement to those earnest combatants of the drink evil who labour in. Warrington. There was a large attendance of the more thought- ful members of the community and a spirit of genuine satisfaction was manifested at the pro- posals for temperance reform embodied in the Government Licensing Bill. The half-loaf princi- ple is now gmerally accepted among temperance reformers as being a doctrine of sound common , sense, and it is a matter for congratulation that they are content to accept what is offered piece- meal in the right direction rather than squabble for an almost unattainable ideal. Some day, no doubt, even the ideal will be reached, but it can only be by small stages and we must all be satis- fied to hasten slowly in the direction of lessening the drink evil. The new Bill deals largely with the regulation and management of drunkards, who, as Canon Hicks remarked,need both. Should the Bill become law, and all temperance advocates sincerely hope that it will,then the man or woman who gets "over the line " and ventures into a public place while thus intoxicated will be liable to prosecu- tion. This clause alone should do much to lessen drunkenness, for a large percentage of the people who can be seen any night staggering homeward —probably quite peacefully—will take care to drink less when they recognise their liability to be convicted of an offence against the law. Drunk- ards in change of children will be summarily arrested in the interests of the child, while married People will be given the opportunity of , separation should either husband or wife desire it 'on account of the insobriety of the other. The registration of clubs is also a step in the right direc- tion. These are, roughly, the proposals contained in the new bill, which is a most important and valuable measure. The benefits likely to accrue from it will transcend even those already the out- come of the Children's Bill, and we -sincerely hope that it will pass the ordeal of the Lords and Com- mons without suffering any serious mutilation. The Borough Member failed last year to support the Children's Bill. lie has now an opportunity of mending his ways and showing that he is fully alive to the necessities of the age. This is not class legislation, and there will be no excuse for Mr R Pierpoint, M.P. if he fails to vote for the licensing proposals of the Government he reckons to support. PUBLIC STINEFIT TRTDBR viz CLOCK Poll BEST BOOTS IN LAMA</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>E EXA.MIN ER Fill PA I' APRIL 25, 1902.</head>
        <p>E EXA.MIN ER Fill PA I' APRIL 25, 1902.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR R. ABEL.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday at the Runcorn cemetery were in- terred the remains of the late Mr Richard Abel. Deceased, who was a shipping owner, had for 801110 time been in failiog health and died on Sunday last at his residence, Crofton Lodge. Deceased was a very popular man, and his death at the age o 56 caused a great amount of sorrow in the neighbour- hood. There were a large number of mourners, and marks of respect were everywhere visible as the ody was move I to its last resting place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Peace negotiations, in which so many heads of families in all parts of the country feel directly and personally concerned, on account of absent ones or :hose but recently returned, like Aaron's rod, has swallowed up. all the interest felt in newspaper reading during the past week. But it is highly probable that three weeks more will elapse before the final decision is arrived at. Until then the newspapers will continue to be voted dull by a majority of the public. Meanwhile, many persons are asking what the conditicasts submitted by Lord Kitchener to General Botha about a yeir ago, to which so many references are now made, really were. Hence it may be as well to mention here that as the result of unconditional surrender, there was to be complt-te amnesty for the burghers; prisoners were to be allowed to return at once; a Crown Colony was to be established at the earliest possible date; and Dutch and English were to be on a footing of equality. Besides this the British Government offered to replace farmers on their farms, and restore their buildings. The Boers were not to be specially taxed, and were to be given a million sterling to meet tue debt incurred during the war. The Boers were also to be allowed the use of sporting arms. The Kaffirs were to be pro- tected, but should not have the franchise. What the precise terms are that are now being offered is not allow,d to transpire, but they are understood to be upon the lines here indicated.</p>
        <p>An encouraging hint given in the "Daily Tele- rrraph " by the well-known war correspondent, Mr Bennett Burleigh, has during the week been made much of. Cultivating that habit of observation and deduction which" Mr Sherlock Holmes" ha 4 pope- lanced, the famous correspondent tells his readers that, the Boer delegates, before leaving Pretoria on their peace mission, ordered supplies of clothing an i of groceries, not, it is to be understood, for commando, but for their private residences. The inference, of course, is clear The men who ordered thee goods expect to be able shortly to enjoy them ; for they were to be " delivered early," and to :enjoy them at their own homes. The delegates know at least as much as anybody else can know what the prospects are, and they, apparEntly, do not expect to be exiled. The fact stated is import- ant. It makes it clear that the Goverment have given some assurance to the Boer delegres that if they surrender they will escape the penalty of ex- patriation which would otherwise hang over them</p>
        <p>Not long have the public had to wait for the effscts, which mot of us certainly expected, of the Chancellor of the ExchPquer's proposal for a corn duty. Th.se elf ,cts are abundantly manifest in the list of market reports appearing in the daily papers from all parts of the country. In rawly towns and cities the price of the quartern loaf has been raised a halfpenny, and in some cases the advance is larger. At Canterbury corn market wheat values b catne two shillings higher on Saturday than in the previous week, while at Peterborough. which is an important centre,farmers h ld their stocks lathe Pxpectation of a further advance.ln most provincial markets, if not all, there has been a prevalent dis- location of prices, while the farmers complain that though the Government impost on cereals was in their favour, the tax on manufactured cereals, which include feeding stuffs for the cattle, will more than counter-balance the gain. As a well- known expert at one of the big markets said, the Government proposals really amount to giving the farmer a shilling with one hand and takicg from him two shillings with the other. Thus the minimum of auvantage has been offered, while the maximum of irritation has already been realised.</p>
        <p>With regard to the Consumer, it can be truly said that his outlook is distinctly discouraging and unfaveurable. All the cereal breakfast foods which have attained so much popularity in this country will be fractionally dearer. The loaf will cost more; and the price of meat is everywhere going steadily up. in consequence, it is " alleged as they say in the police courts—of the operations ot a certain American Trust It is well that the summer is be- fore us, when these niggling little taxes may not be so sharply felt as might otherwise be the case But when the winter comes the pinch will become more appreciable among the very poor, whose margin of expenditure on the necessaries of life is always cut down to the smallest possible limit. Sir W Flarcourt the other night rubbed in the lessons of the financial criticisms passed upon the Budget, and especially the" insignificant tax upon cheques and the pernicious tax upon corn." The objection that non-political students of finance prefer against these taxes is not only that they are irritat- ing and offensive themselves, but that they are needless, in so much as other sources of revenue which had been abandoned might have been re- vived, and the uthers have been as yet untouched.</p>
        <p>It is curious to find the 'Birmingham Daily Past,' ten spoken of as "Chamberlain's paper," declaring that the Government have in several of their pro- posals " strained the allegiance of many of their followers to the utmost ; " so staunch a Unionist as Sr Walter Thorburn telling the Chancellor of the Exchequer that it " strained his loyalty " to be asked to put a tax on the staple food of the people; and Mr Alex Crosq, Liberal Unionist, M P for Glas- gow, as one entitled to speak for a great trading community, saying he specially condemned the corn tax, as it broke up the simplicity of our fiscal system by adding about forty articles to the tariff."</p>
        <p>It appears that the recent official inquiry into the manufacture of butter in this country bee resulted in something which is considered practical being done to fix the standard of that commodity. For the future any specimen containing more than 16 per cent of water may be condemned by the authorities under the Sale of noi and Drugs Act, a measure to which the "ionsumer owes a great deal. It is a source of satisfaction that a standard has thus been fixed, since farmers, butter merchants, and other dealers will now know where they stand. But this is not to be understood as implying that the British public ought to be well pleased with such a standard, To pay butter price for even 16 per cent only of that which is not butter, but water, is far from satis- factory. Still the step taken is in the right direc- tion The regulation which the Board of Agriulture published on Tuesday on the subject, may entail a little trouble on butter manufacturers, who may now be called upon t 3 guarantee that their butter does not contain a higher percentage of water than that mentioned. But eventually, no doubt, any in- convenience of the kind will disappear to a very great extent.seeing butter factories at some time or other must become popular if the co-oneretive system gains ground, as it is hoped it Will,through- out the country. If anybody deserves to be sym- pathised with in this connection it is the pur- chaser and consumer of the butter—and water. Experts tell ug that there is no need to have six- teen per cent of water in the butter, but as it will be some improvement on the systAm hitherto in vo7,ue we must " rest and be thankfur—for the present.</p>
        <p>lk,S. ASHTON'S LIFR TONTO TS THE KING OF TONICS —63. SANKEY-STREW, Warrington.—(Advt.) LADTIt3 HRST LACM BOOTS AND SHO UND 3R THY. CLOCK, BRIDO-H-STREBT.—Advt. MOTOR BIOYOLR3.—The "B‘vllas Thom ta Rxnel- elor "Sole Agent. J s Ge.nr.strn,Britige-st, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Primrose Day at Warrington. Saturday being the anniversary of the death of the Earl of Beaconsfield,a, large wreath of primroses wag hung outside the Conservative Club in Sankey- street. A similar wreatb was displayed in front of the Junior Congervatiire Club in Orford-lane, and each of the members was presented with a button- hole. In the streets the flower was generally worn. M ASHTON'S LINN TONTO is ram KING OF TONICS.-63. SiNKEY-MREET Warrintoll.—CAdvt</p>
      </div>
