      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>STEALING A LAD-1)Iin.</head>
        <p>STEALING A LAD-1)Iin.</p>
        <p>At the Frodsham Police Court on Wednesday, James Wilson, Runcorn, was charged with stealing a step ladder the property of Dr Selby, on the 9-. la inst. The evidence showed that the prisoner bor- rowed a ladder from Emily Lightfoot, domestic servant at the prosecutor's, saying he would return the same after he hal finished a joti of painting.— As prisoner did not return information was given to the police, who f mud that the ladder had been sold to a man named Lewis at Kingsley. Prisoner was committed tor fourteen days. _</p>
        <p>ST. JOIIN AMBULANCE ASSOC' lATION-RUNCORN</p>
        <p>BRANCII.</p>
        <p>At the examination held at the Technical Insti- tute, on Wednesday,April 9th, the following candi- dates were successful :—Re-examination : W Ash- brook, W Booth, W Clare, T Carson, W Ford, Ponce Constable Gilpin, R Gibbs, J Harrison, R L Hughes, J Leach, T Murphy, and G Walton. Final re- examination : A Cslion, C Crawford, Joseph Clare, G T Duckworth,Polics-Constables Edwards,Gregory and J Wilding, W Gregory, G Hill, R W Harrop, Joseph Lightfoot,W Ray, P Rowlinson, and ASteel. Dr MacLennan was the examiner. SONS OF TEMPERANCE —THE ROSE OF HALTON DIVISION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above society was held on Saturdays evening in the Village Hall, under the presidency of Mr Fred Ilankey. There was a good attendance of officers and members, and a number of visitors were present from Runcorn. After the routine business had been transacted, Mr Thomas Cooke, P,GI.W P., of Runcorn, in an impressive manner, installed the newly-elected officers of the division. Mr F Hankey (the chairman) said that he would do what he coul I to ad vance the best in- terests of the division, and thus serve the cauee of temperance.—Mr Thomas Cooke, on behalf of Mr Thomas Heesom, of Runcorn, presented a complete I Set of beautiful badges for the officers of the divi- sion. and invested each officer with his distinctive decoration. He spoke in complimentary terms of Mr Heesom's generosity and his attachment to the order. Mr C P Bradbury, in a few well-chosen words, moved that the best thanks of the members be given to Mr lieesom for his timely gift. This was seconded by Mr Thomas Hughes, and carried with acclamation.—Mr Heesom replied,and said he rejoiced at the fact that they had opened a division in Halton, and he wished it much success. Toe following officers and members took part in the meeting: —Messrs Daniel Bisbrown, Thomas J Holt Alfred Nodal). Thomas R Whyte, Samuel Tomlinson, sen., Samuel Tomlinson, jun., and Joseph Astbury. It is proposed to open a cadet section of which kir William Looker will be the patron, and Mr William liankey, the secretary</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>lIALTox Pirasu COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>lIALTox Pirasu COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Council was held on 1 Thursday, under the presidency of Mr Moores. On the motion of Mr Hughes, seconded by Mr Hough- ton, Mr A Povey, the vice-chairman, was appointed chairman of the Council for the ensuing twelve months. Mr Posey thereupon took the chair, and returned thanks for election. —Mr J B Houghton was elected vice-chairman.— A letter was read from Mr R P Ward, organizing secretary to the Technical Instruction Committee of the County Council, furnishing particulars with regard to lectures by the specialists apoointed by the Council. The list for the current season was complete, and the best course for the Parish Council to take, if they desired a course of lectures during the next winter, was to make their application before the end of August.—lt was resolved, on the motion of Mr Moores, seconded by Mr Barrow, that t he Chairman, Vice-Chairman, Clerk and Air Jeacock, form a committee to select suitable subjects for lectures from the list submitted by Mr Ward. SI -sere L3oker and Asti:lm.y were appointed over- seers. The clerk read a latter from Mr George F Ashton, clerk to the Rural District Council, enclos- ing copy of letter he had received from Messrs Robert Davies and Company, solicitors, Warrington with reference to the road near Halton Geange. After perusing the estate deeds and plans they (the solicitors) thought the Parish Council were entirely in error in claiming that there was ever a public right of road over the land in question. There had never been any such right exercised or claimed since the late Charles Hazlehurst purchased the Grange about 30 years ago, and the deeds and plans supported them in their view that no such right of way ever existed. With reference to Mr Ashton's remarks about the tithe map, it surely did not follow, because the Surveyor who was employed to prepare the map took upon himself to describe a road as a bridle path, that it was, therefore, a pub- lic road, and they (the solicitors) could not agree that the tithe map was admissible as evidence on the point. Resolved that the matter be left in the 'hands of the Footpaths Committee with a view to further steps being taken to ascertain if there was a public right of way. —Mr Jeacock announce I that Sir Richard hi Brooke,Bart., had intimated his intention to enter- tain 'all the children attending the day schools on Coronation day at the Priory. The majority of the people of Halton could collect sufficient funds to make a:jollification among themselves. He did not see that the Parish Council were called upon to do anything more than entertain the old people. lie thought the others would be willing to do some- thing for themselves. He suggested that a parish meeting should be convened at an early date to consider and decide as to the bet way of cele- brating the auspicious event, and how to obtain funds. They might embrace the whole of the ecclesiastical parish in the invitation—that would include Stockham,Norton, and Rock Savage. He did not agree witn taking money out of the rates for this purpose, unless it was absolutely necessary for entertaining the poor. The Council, after some discussion, resolved to call a parish meeting at an early date to consider the form in which the event should be celebrated in the village.—MrJames Tom- linson was appointed caretaker of the village all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>~-..-. LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES,</head>
        <p>~-..-. LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES,</p>
        <p>The Regular Daily Sa.ilings of the L verpool and Nirth Wales Steamship Company,Ltd..will begin on Saturday, May 3rd, when the `• Snowdon" will leave the Prince's Landing Stage at 10.45 *.m. for Llandudno and Beau- marls, arriving back at 7.15 p.m. There will be no con- nection for Bangor or M-nai Bridge until Friday, 16:11 May, when the " St Tadao" will take up the regular daily sailings at 10.45 a.m., to LllnJudno, Be.aunoaris, Bangor and Menai Bridge, due back at 7.30 p.m. At Whitiuntide the Sailing' will be considprably in- creased, as in addition to the "St Elvis's "at 9.15 a.m., on Whit-Monday, the "St Tudno " will leave Liverpool at 10.45 a.m., for Llandudno and Holyhead, which is certainly one of the most eojlyable of the trips out of Liverpool. At L'anduino passengers can transfer to the "Snowdon " for Beaumaris, Menai Bridge, Rill Carnarvon, calling at all places on the return journey, and arriving back ire Liverpool about 8 p.m. Frequently during the latter part of May and June Afternoon Excursion 3 will be run by the " Sc. Elvies "and " Snowdon." It may be interesting to state that the catering is under the contrrl of the Company, so that passengers by any of the steamers may be assured of satisfactory treatment. The special terms for Season Tickets and Family Contract Tickets are most reasonable, and it will be found that in many eases a very considerable saving may be effected by taking the advantages offered in this respect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Warrington Man Final for Travelling Without</head>
        <p>Warrington Man Final for Travelling Without</p>
        <p>Ticket.</p>
        <p>At Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday, before Mr T J Gillespie (chairman), R Stone, W Coiling- wood. and J Unsworth, John Bond Tanner, of War- rington, pleaded guilty to travelling from St. Helens Junction to Warrington, on the London and North- Western Railway, without having previously paid his fare.—Mr Fenna, of Liverpool, prosecuted, and said that on March 4th defendant was seated in a train at ten minutes past nine, at Earlestown Junc- tion. The ticket examiner went to him and asked for his ticket. Defendant said he had just got in, and produced a return half of a workntsn's ticket from Earlestown to Warrington. The examiner was doubtful, and called the attention of the guard to him, and defendant afterwards admitted that he hail travelled from St. Helens Junction. He offered to pay at Warrington, but it was not taken.— Ticket-examiner Noble gave evidence as to the facts.—Tanner was fined !Os and costs. UNDBR THE CLOCK Fort GOOD BOOTS.—Adv " L1&amp;-FRANCIS" CYCLIS.-7Britaln s scknowledged</p>
        <p>best. Sole ^Agent, GARLAND, Bridge•st, Warrington. 30,000 restoredßLN f.) PbIrmPELLTILIIIRa. v1e6137,; xthioericir .sistlrgehott Manchester. The most successful Eye Specialist in England. He has cured after Hospitals, Doctors, and Oculists have failed Book post free on mentioning this pap er.—A4vt.</p>
      </div>
