      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>MODEL YACHING.</p>
        <p>The first race of the season in connection with the Runcorn Mc,del Yacht Club was held on Satur- day on toe Weaver Canal.—There were 24 entries, but owing no doubt to the windy weather which prevailed the competition did not attact a large attendance. The competitors assembled at the bridge at 2.30, end the race was started at 3 p.m ,to the accompaniment of a etiff breeze, which fairly tested the quality of the boats. The prizes were three copper kettles, and these were awarded as follows: 1, Mr T liathbone's Lorna; 2, Mr J lox- worth's Joey ; 3,, Mr 11 Lightfoot's Albion. Each prize-winner was presented with a flag and decor- ated his yacht.</p>
        <p>WESTON PARISIIICOUNCIL. The annual meting was held on Friday evening at the Cnurcla schools, Weston under the presidency ot the Rev Canon Maitland Wood. On the motion of Mr Basnett, seconded by Mr J Griffiths, the Rev Canon Wood was unanimously re-elected chairman On the motion of Mr Tudor, seconded by Mr Bas nett, Messrs T Ford, sea ; and A G Goes were re-appointed overseers of the poor. On the motion of Mr Guest, seconded by Mr Bamber, Mr Shaw was unanimously appointed treasurer. A letter was read from Mr Reginald Potts, clerk to the Cheshire County Council, invit- ing the Parish Council to subscribe towards the local memorial to her late Majesty, Queen Victoria, which is to take the form of a statue of her late Majesty, to be erected in Chester. Towards this it was proposed that the county should contribute £l,OOO and the City of Chester .£5OO. The sum al- ready subscribed in the county was £598 2s 91.— Resolved that the letter lie on the table.</p>
        <p>AMA.LGABIATED UNION OF CO-OPRRATIVR Erd- PLoYEs, Runom AND .WIDNEi BRANCH. .</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the above was held in the office of the Runcorn Co-operative Society. Ditton- road, Widnes, on Tue-day, Mr J H. Cooper presiding. At the conclusion of the ordinary business Mr Charles Hill of Runcorn was called upon to present to Mr Wesley Coventry, branch secretary, on behalf of the members a Swan fountain pen. In rising to do so,he referred to the time devoted and the sacri- fices made by Mr Coventry in order to attend all the meetings, and wished him health and strength to work amongst them. Mr Coventry, in accepting the gift, spoke of the pleasure it had given him to do all he coul 1 for his fellow-employes. And while feeling that his services had been appreciated, as was manifest that night in a very tangible manner, he could not take all toe praise and honour, for there were those present who had urged upon theie employes that unity was strength and begged of them to support their own union. The recipient then gave a detailed report of the progress of the Branch since its formation in 1898 and urged upon those present not to rest satisfied until every employe became a member. The following gentle- men also spoke—Messrs J F Elolroyd (Widnes), J II Cropper (Widnes), W Pritchard (Runcorn), and J Haynes (Widnes).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>COUPLE OF SOLDIERS IN TROUBLE.</head>
        <p>A COUPLE OF SOLDIERS IN TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>At the Frodsham Police Court on W ednesday before Messrs A Thomas and J Murray Thomas Finn and George Andrews, two discharged soldiers were charged with begging in Bridge-street on the 14th April. They were further charged with stealing two stable rubbers valued at 3s belonging to Thomas Sand on the same date.—The evidence showed that on the date in question the prisoners went to Dr Selby's house and wanted to see him. When he went t 3 the door they were gone and looking through the door he saw them standing by Nfr Sand's door. The prisoners then want into Mr Sand's shop and offered some laces for sale. Nothing was missed out of the shop at the time but on Mr Sand being asked by the police whether he had missed anything two stable rubbers were missing. Sergeant Runt had arrested the prisoners on a coarge of ba,gging and on hearing the facts of the case further charged them with stealing the rubbers.—On the same date prisoners were proved to have been begging of several persons. On the charge of stealing Finn was committed for two months, and for begging fourteen days. Andrews was committed for 14 days for begging and one month for stealing. FUNERAL OF THE LATE Mn JOSEPH BELL._</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the remains of the late Mr Joseph Ball were interred in the Runcorn Cemetery. Deceased had been in the service of the Hizlehurst family for over 40 years as coaciman Though he had been in failing health some time his death was entirely unexpected as he had followed his duties right to the time of his decease. Deceased was in his Gist year. The cortege left the Grange about 2 30.. Preceding the hearse, were:—Rev Alfred Wells (supt Wesleyan minister), Messrs J Hancock, W Faulkner, W Grice, W Hulme, H Walton, George Bold, J Joyce.E Gregory,R Morris,J Horsefield,J Woodcock,F Smith, P Rice, J Houghton, T Blundell, J Williamson, II Helslo, James Reid, S Percival, R W Harrop, T Caygill, and W Welch. T.le bearers were the gar- deners on the e4tate, viz, Messrs Stirton, J Hardy, C Speed, and H Moss. Next to the hearse walked the mourners :—Messrs William, Harry, and Charlie Ball (sons), James, Thomas, and William Bell (brothers) ; Mrs Taylor, Mrs Leah, and Miss Ellen Ball (sistersl; Mr C S Kniveton (son-in-law); Masters Frank Kniveton and Cyril Bell (grandsons) ; Mr James Garner and Mr James Taylor (brothers-in-law) ; Mrs Charles Hazleburst's carriage, containing Mr Raynor, Master Edward Raynor, and Mr J H Salkeld : and in the rear were Mc George Acton (farmer on the estate), and Messrs E lieesom, T Corker and T Peers, work- men. On arrival at the cemetery the burial service was impressively conducted by the Rev Alfred Wells in the presence of a large number of sympathizing friends. The coffin was literally enveloped with wreaths contributed by:—" Mother and Nellie," Willie and Ada, Harry and Charlie, Charlie (Mr C Kniveton), and Lucy, Willie and Edith, deceased's three sisters; brother Tom and wife, Mrs Charles Hazlehurst, Mr and Mrs C W Hazlehurst (Hereford),Miss Hazlehurst, Mr and Mrs Raynor (Mossley Hill), Master and the Misses Raynor, the indoor servants at the Grange, deceased's fellow workmen, Mrs Brimelow, Mr and Mrs Ball, Messrs J and J W Joyce. . CAMDEN WESLEYAN CHAPEL, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A cantata entitled " Spring Flowers," or " The May Queen's Garden," was given in the schoolroom of the above chapel on Monday evening, by the members of the Band of Hope. The chair was occupied by the Rev J B Foster. The characters represented were ;—May Queen, Mies E Duncalf ; attendants, Misses L Inman and J Santley ; Hebe, Miss F Watton, Forestus, Mr F Kearsley ; Monitora, Miss M Abrams; gardener's children, Mabel Holt, Carrie Challoner, Wesley Fleet, and Thomas E Bell; flowers, Miriam Bell, Jessie Davies, Nellie Worrall, Lily Astbury, Nellie Derbyshire, Leah Britland, Robert Abrams, Robert Bennison, J Bazley. E Dut- ton and Jacob Abrams ; buttercups and daisies, infants. The children wore pretty costumes, sug- gestive of the character they depicted. The cantata was composed of solos and choruses, interspersed with recitations and dialogues, The accompanist was Mr Walter Littler. At the conclusinni of the performance Mrs J B Forster distributed prizes to the following scholars for at- tendance during the past year ;—William Dutton Alfred Santley.Ernest Hughes,Jane Lambert, Ewart Bell, Emma Dean, Ernest Fleet, John Hughes, Thomas Wilson, Arthur Wilson, Joseph Fleet,Ernest Dutton, Percy Dutton, Florence Bennett, Annie Wilson, Wesley Fleet, Henry Hedgecock, Florence Lambert, Mabel Fleet, Harry, Samuel and William Davies, T Clarkson, T Hughes, Isaac Heath, Ethel Dodd, H Bloat, R Hedgecock, A Bibby, L Bell, T Booth, J Dean, C Bennison, A Rowe, Lily Astbury, , Mary J Molt, Miriam Bell, Florence Watton, W Lambert, J Robinson, C Gandy, R Gandy, A Robin- son, Harriet Heath, Jane Watton, J Walker, John T Summerfield. J Martin, W Martin, H Bennison, T Watton, L Btitland, Mabel Holt,li Martin, T Evans, Nellie Temperley, Frederick Ashmore, Dorothy Temperley,Lizzie Martin,Bessie Walker, F Harrison, J Walker, Eva Booth, r Martin, M Jane, and Emma Harrison. Florence Maddock, II Hayes, G Hignett, Bennison, J and George Hayes, and Elsie Harri- son.</p>
        <p>=Mal</p>
      </div>
