      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>A very amusing incident occured the other day which shows the vigilance exercised by the Run- corn police. On Sunday evening P.C. Wilding, stationed on duty in C lurch-street, saw a vagradt rooster parading about in an aimless fashion and apparently without visible means of subsistence. With a strict sense of duty the worthy constable promptly gave chase, and after an exciting sprint duly took into custody the errant fowl. Arriving at the police station, followed by s. large crawl, ha accordingly locked his prisoner up in one of the cells, presumably to appear belore the bench in due course. A fitting climax to the comedy was, however, spoiled by the arrival of the owner on the sc-ne, aMr W Pollen, coal dealer In a heated manner he veaemently declared that the rooster belonged to im, an that he could give indisputable proofs that such was the case. On I being taken to the cell he called out a pet name aith which the rooster was evidently well ac- quainted. for he set up a lusty crow, and, recognis- ing his master, flew to his sheltering embrace for protection from the minions of the law. The proof was considered sufficient and the prisoner was dis- charged, it is further stated that the prisoner was cautioned not to again be found wandering in the public streets, otherwise he would be treated as a vagrant and subjected to all the pains and penalties of the law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly me-ting of this Board on Tuesday at Daton Workhouse there were present Mersra J S:.okes, A R Norman, J.P., C.C., T Wosd, the members :of tha Rural Connell, witn Me4ar, Or F A+hton (c erk), W E Elou.:11 (deputy cl&gt;rk),and J Cuanah (master). Mr J S.Lokes was tinammouoly elec•,ed as chairman for the ensuing ear, and the honour of viee-3hairman devolved upon Mr A R ziorrnan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>FIRE ESCAPES.</head>
        <p>FIRE ESCAPES.</p>
        <p>Mr W FCnollys,assistant seeret,ry to the Local Govern- ment B ,Ard, wrote stating that the latter had approved under their seal the plans of the proposed fire—soaps stairs at the Runcorn Union Workhouse. The plans were returned, and i 4 was recp,-,sted that the B Jard might be furnished with copies on tracing cloth for de- posit at their office. Th Board sanctioned an expendi- ture of £2OO on the work,And in regar I t ) rhi pr ,p)sed loan considered it should be repaid in 20 years, floe Clerk iicl he had forwarded copies of th plans, and.' h took it he could now instruct the Lion Foundry Company to proceed with the work. Toe Chairman : Yes (hat is se. THE COHON&amp;TION.</p>
        <p>Mr S B Provis, seor-tary the Local Gov4Triment Bard, wrote to the effect that the 26ta and 27. h days of June next had been appointed as public holidays in connection with the Coronation of Rh Majesty the K ng, and th 4 B)ar.i in accordann. with rrprsseutaams made to tnera bad issued an order enabling the Bevel al Boards of Guardians, Joint Committees of sac Boards and Boards of Management to make such notifications in the regulations in force with regard to the discipline and diet of the poor pe-sons in the W &amp;c , as might seem to them suitable for the occasion. The Order contemplated that the event should be celebra- ted in those institutions on the days appointed as pub- lic holidays, or on such other day or days not exceeling two in number as might be determined by the various bodies,snd authorised reasonable expenditure in connec- tion therewith. TnA Order also provided for reasonable additions being made t out-loor relief in the week including.the.26 h June. . . . .</p>
        <p>It was-decide I that ttp Clerk ob'ain information reg. pecking toe payment of out-7eAef at the Dia-nond Jubllse, and the other matter was relvated to tite ilouse Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>FUTURE OF WORKHOUSE BOYS.</head>
        <p>THE FUTURE OF WORKHOUSE BOYS.</p>
        <p>The Cl,rk read a tarter from 51: A Weil.sley Brcainer of the training ship Indefatigahle in whieh he said they always looked after boys on the;r return from sea, and they had instructions when leaving to report themselves on arrival, in fact they weirs net discharged from their indentures until evidence was forthcoming that thee were able to look after themselvei. Thee kept a record oi them and could give intormation about their where3bouts any time for the first twelve months. Some of them did not vist the ship but thee generally heard of them from the owners and if unfavourably he brought them back and made them srsrve some months longer. He thought all the Board's boys were well abls to lo ok after themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>PAUPER LONLTICS.</head>
        <p>PAUPER LONLTICS.</p>
        <p>Aletter was read from Mr LI tiornhy, clerk to the Chester County Asylum Committee intimating that the rate of maintenance of pauper lunatics in the asylum for the quarter ending June 30. h was fixed at 7s 71 p3r he ad pn week. It was pointed out by the clerk that this was the same in amount as the previous charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>NATIONAL HELP FOR SOLDIERS AND THEIR FAMILIES,</head>
        <p>NATIONAL HELP FOR SOLDIERS AND THEIR FAMILIES,</p>
        <p>A circular letter from the Biackburn Uuion was submitted in which the support of the Board was re- quested in regard to the blowing r solution passed by tnem:—"That this Board is of opinion that all Boards ot Guardians throughout the country should petition the Cnancellor of the Exchequer to at once take the nsces- sary steps, to provide, from nation ti sources, for the adPcoate maintenance of the wives and children of soldiers now in South Africa ; also for all disabled soldiers and their families, and for the widows and chil- dren of all soldiers who have died in consequence of in- juries rec dved or disease contracted during the present Boer war,"</p>
        <p>Oa the motion of Mr W, ol seclude 1 by Mr Rlylsoce it was resolved to support IMP resolution.</p>
        <p>THE RESULT OF THE BUUGF:T.</p>
        <p>Mr E Gregory, Bridge-,reet, wrot. asking if the Board would allow him say ad per dczel loaves on bread, leper sack on fl mr and oatmeal. although he had bought (forward) fl )ur to cover his contract, be had to pay an additional Is per sack duty. M.ssrs J Fairelough and Sons Lunited,forwarded 4 circular stating in consequence of the duty on wheat, and flour, all con- tracts made prior to 15th April which had not been completed before that date would te charged duty on the undelivered portion as and when delivered by add- ing an amount for same as a separate item on the invoice which was to be calculated at the rate of 5d per 11211).. Ot was agreed on the motion of Mr Woodward toat the application be granti'd, but that in future the Clerk insert upoo the form of contract • clause that in the event of duties being reduced the go)ds should be pro- portionately reduced.</p>
