      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Proposals for Earlestown and Newton.</head>
        <p>Proposals for Earlestown and Newton.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the committee appointrd by the District Council for carrying out the local celebrations in connec- tion with the forthcoming Coronation, was held at tee Town Hall, Eirlestown, on Tuesday evening. Mr W Collingwood preided,and there were presect—Messrs E I' Evans, W Gilmour, J Simpson, N Siddeley. Dr Patter- son, and Mr John Latham, representing the Friendly Societies. Alter some discussion it was decided b pub- licly celebrate the Coronation on June 26 h. It was suggested that there be a procession of the Trades and Friendly Societies, school children, and the boys from the Liverpool Farm School Reformatory; that fourpence per head be allowed each school on the average attend- ance in order to provide a treat for the children, and that each child be presented with a commemoration cup or mug. If funds allowed the aged people would bc entertained and there would be a fireworks display. If fuel could be obtained gratis a bonfire would be lighted. It was decided to ask Lord Newton to allow the use of the Football ground for the purpose of mustering the procession. Another meeting will be bed next week, at which representatives of the various Trade and Friendly . Societies will be a4ted to attend.</p>
      </div>
