      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EDUCATION BILL. Councillor Sennett Sees " Noth!ng to be Alarmed About."</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION BILL. Councillor Sennett Sees " Noth!ng to be Alarmed About."</p>
        <p>Speaking at the annual meeting of the Warrington Polling District of the N3arton Division Conservative Association, held at Paclgate on 511nday evening, Coun- cillor Bennett referred to the Government's Blucation Bill. He said he was a Weileyasi, and speaking as one he did not see that there was anything to be alarmed about. It was not an altogether ideal Bill, There were things in it which Fe would like to have seen otherwise. Be was not a believer in sectarian education, but the church schools had for generations done the bulk of the educational work of the coutrry and wou'd only receive their due. If the Nonconformists desired to avail them- selves of the opportunities which the Bill offered they would have every chance. They had only to erec,. their own schools and they would be able to otrain supoort from the rate. He said this because some of his Non- conformist friends se-rnel to be unduly alarmed about the The control of education might with advant- age be centred in county councils and borough councils, who would by a provision nf the Bat be abl3 to elect educational experts from outside.</p>
      </div>
