        <p>• SMALL-PC) IC HOSPITAL, MOORE. Messrs Dunlop, Wallis, and Co., agents to the Ship Canal Company, wrote under date April 10. h stating th‘t they would aeree to let the Council the above hospital on a yearly t nancy. Fu'Aect to six months' notice at the annual rent of £lO, toe tenancy to date from March 25. h. , The clerk pointed that the amount had been reduced from £2O to £lO. MR PEMBERTON'S THREATENED APPEAL. Mr John Pember on, of Heathcot, Stockton Heath, writing with reference to tis appeal ye the Pike-street property, said it could not be denied that in St )ckton Heath hearths had always been and are now constructed exactly in the same way that his were. N)t only so,but he could bring abundant evidence that they were so- cmnstruc el in Manche:ter, Liverpool, and Warrineton. He had no wish to evade any bye-law, but there were so many of them that had never been enforced that builders did not know wha-, to do. However, if the bye- laws were going t be enforced all round he could not complain, but thq Council would have to deal with the builders of every piece of property in Stock'on Heath I since the bye-laws were passed, tie contended that tbere was something unreasonable about the bye-laws and they wereidisregarded by everybody with impunity except in his own case. Decided that. the Clerk reply that the Council could not entertain his request for an interview with the ICommittee.</p>
