      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>NEW VOLUNTEER REGULATIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NEW VOLUNTEER REGULATIONS.</p>
        <p>Training and Camps.</p>
        <p>The Volunteer regulations approved by the Secretary for War resvectisg the conditions of efficiency for officers and Volunteers were is wed on Tuesday night, with a special Army order of this date, In order Co he reekon.d as efficient and to qualify for the efficiency rant of 353, the officer muLt comply with the following conditions, 'according t his rank or ap- pointment : A field officer must attend the annual iniqyction and camp or the number of regimental or battalion parades presented for the trained Volunteer of his arm of the service. A battery or company officer must fulfil with his unit the coaditions oresaribed for the Volunteer recruit or trained man of hia arm of the service.</p>
        <p>A Volunteer in order to be reckoned as efficient and to qualify for the efficiency grant of 35, most, unless exempted, attend camp and comply with the condi- tions prescribed for his arm of the service. Artillery recruits' training for gunners will consist of at !Peet forty—five attendances of not less than one hour each, at least twenty being made before the company battery or Company. _ .</p>
        <p>Training for trained officers and Vllunteers will con- sist of at least fifteen attendances of not less than one hour each. Regimental camps will only be held when specially authorised by the Commander-ir-Chief. They will not be sanctioned unless two previous attendances have beets at brigade camps or camps with regular forces.</p>
        <p>_ _ Regimental camps will be held by garrison artillery corps at workq of defence, supplied with modern arma- ment, every third year Garrison artillery corps will camp at the fortress, 8;3, allotted to it on mobilisation, unless exempted fcr a special reason by the general officer commanding. Eogineemothor than sub-marine miners and eictritial eneineeta, recruits' training will consiEt of forty-five attendances of an hour each. -</p>
        <p>Company training for trained officers and Volunteers will cßnsist of fifteen attendances of an bour each.</p>
        <p>Every third Tetr at least engineer corps will camp at a fortress or work ef defence unlees specially exempted. Darin.' other years it will camp at a station suitable for carrying out engineering duties. Work in camp will be mainly confined t) instructon in such engineering, duties as would be performPd by corps on mobilisation, Infantry recruits' training will consist of forty attend- ances of an hour each.</p>
        <p>Company trlining for trained officcr3 and Volunteers will consist of ten att.nd&amp;ncee.</p>
        <p>. _ The training of Volunteer corns in petce will he goinrsed by what they are required to do in war. Instruction for infantry should include scouting and reconnaissance. skirmishing, attack and defence, out- posts by day anA night, advanced and rear •;uards, escorts for guns or convoys, minor tactical schemes of company versus company, bait 'lion versus battalion. blank ammunition., and distinguishing marks by oppos- ing forces thon!el invariably be used. AU exercises should conclude with a conference, at which officers awl non-commissioned officers should be encouraged to explain any action they may have taken. Commanding officers should seldom interfere. Even if subordinate leaders are making mistakes, unless a mistake is of a character to render the instruction abortive, exercise should proceed, and mistakes bo pointed out at a sub- sequent conference. Specral attention must be devoted to the training of officers and non-oommissionel officers to aot on their own initiative, and accept responsibilitc. They should frequent'y be placed in a position requiring prompt action and ready resource. For example, the sudden introduction into a fight of a superior foree, laving of anVeusb, or other surprise, counte: attack, should be improvised.</p>
        <p>M. Trepoff, chief of the Moscow police, whose life has beon twice attempted recently, has applied for permis- sion to resien his post. Willie Llewellyn, the little boy who WRSI lost near Merthyr, has not been found. Great excitement was soused in the neighbourhood on Monday by a report that he had been discovered at Wombwell, Sheffield, but tbo report proved incorrect.</p>
      </div>
