      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>STREET BETTING AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>STREET BETTING AT WAR- RINGTON.</p>
        <p>Maximum Penalty Inflicted. Bookmaker Tries to Swallow a Betting Slip.</p>
        <p>The full penalty was inflicted in a case of street betting which came before the magistrates at the War- ringloa Police Court on Monday. The defendant was William Misnaming, a hairdresser, carrying en haziness at 105, Mersey-street. and he was charged with fre- quenting and using Green-street for the purposes of betting on several dates of this month.—Mr Steel repre- 'seated the defendant and pleaded not guilty.—Mr F Monks was chairman of the Bench. The Chiet Constable said that on April Bth, between neon and one o'clock, r.c Clough was watching the neighbourhood of Greea-street and saw the defendant taking slips from young men and others. Be made entries in a book similar to the one produced whioh was subsequently found in his posseseirao. That was the day of the Warwiek races. On the 16th, he saw him in Green-street at the same time acting in a similar manner That was the data of the Newmarket races. On the 18 11 be watched the defendant taking elites and money from people between 12 20 and 12.40. He made an entry either upon a piece of loose paper in the book or in the book itself. At 12.40 defendant took a slip (pro- duced) from a young man and the officer went towards him. Defendant rushed up Bosteek-street and the officer went after him. Seeing that he would be caught defendant rammed the slip of paper into his mouth and endeavoured to swallow it. When the officer caught him he spat the paper out on the footpath and, so to speak, convicted himself out of his own mouth. The officer, after picking the slip up, told him that he had been watching him and asked him to give him what he bad in his ponession relating to betting. Defendant gave him a be k in which appeared the names of horses which had been running during the week. The names en the slip were the names of horses which ran on that particular day. Having been caught flsgrante &amp;Liao defendant made no denial of the charge of betting and oak] that if the police would look over the matter he would not do it any more. He accempanied Clough as far as Market Gate where they SAW Superintendent Menne/lan. The superinmndent told defendant that be had been watched for some time. Defendant said he had been going there only three or four weeks and had made only five er siX bets that morning. Olough said he had taken more and defendant producing some silver coins from his. pocket said be bad only taken £2. Defendant further said that there was nothing to be got out of barbering, but if they would look over the ease he would give up betting. P.C. Clough gave evidence bearing out this statement He said that when he caught the defendant. he told him (witness) that he usually chewed paper instead of tobacco. (Laughter.) In cross-examination by Mr Steel he denied striking defendant in the face in Bostock-street. Defendant gave him the book voluntarily. Mr Steel said that in the face ef the defendant's ad- mission it would be useless to attempt a defence and he would plead guilty. The Deputy-Mayor said a good deal of interest had been taken in the evidence given before the Committee of the House of Comm ens on the subje :t of betting. He would like to know whether any bets bad been made by women and children in this case. The Cnief Constable said there had been a good deal of it, bul unfortunately he was not able to brine police evidence that defendant had taken bets from wcrratn and children on the dava mentioned. The efficer had told the magistrates all he saw. , Superintendent Monaghan said he bad known the defeedant fur • number of year!. Be was a bookmaker and hairdreeter. He used to take bets in his shop at one time. but found the place was being too closely watched. ' He was in Green-street frequently last summer. The D Tuty Mayor : Have you any reason to believe thet he acts for someooe else ? The Chief Constable: Yes. The Deputy Mayor said it would be worth while exposing the principal by giving the name. The Chief Constable said he did not imagine that the individual in question feared any publicity. He had no direct evidence in which his name could be given.</p>
        <p>The Deputy Mayor : If you can get the names of the principals in th, se csses nu might let us have them. Mr Steel asked the magietrates not Ca inflict the full penalty because it was apt to make a man desperate and set the magistrates an police at fdefianco. If de- fendant ware &amp;lei a lea; amount he midlit decide to give up street betting and all ether betting and be a good citizen. The Chairman Raid the defandent would be fined the full penalty, £5 and costs, or a mouth's imprisonment'. The magistrates were satisfied that it was not his lint offence, nor his second, nor third. The fine with the costs amounted to £5 95.</p>
        <p>STOOPING MEANT GREAT AGONY.</p>
        <p>Stooping mlant grrat agmy to Mr. Thos. Gerry. of 39, East Pcntip Ctiry, Annli:2l.l Plain, Durham. Writing on April 3rd, 1901, he says ;—" I June, last year, while working in the pit, I was unfortunate enough to meet with a serious accidont. my body being cruMed very badly. I was kid up forthree loPeks with this, but found that my kidnoys had bean aced by the accident. I had some pains in the lower /mit of the back and could only do my work with great difficulty ; so much so that when I had to reach the ground I had to go on my bands to do so, as I could not stoop with- out great agony."</p>
        <p>" My water." he adds, " was also thick and muddy, and I begs.o to feel afraid I had received some perma- cent injury. Mr uerry fortunately found a remedy • a reined,: that ttousands of people have found, Dosid's Kidney Pills. "After taking twe orthiee doses," he writes, " I found a great difference in tho water, also in myself." After taking three boxy he says, '•1 feel as well as ever ; tie pain has quite left me, and tiv to is no sign of the trouble in any way." The pain in the back toweyer was not dee to the accident, but to the kidneys. Thousands have back. ache and are tortured with it daily ; thousands suffer great agony when they attempt to stoop, and the cause is the--iamo the kidneys. The cure is a kidney cure, The best of kidney cures is Dodd's Kidney Pills. They cure the kidneys, and when the kidneys are well the waste refuse of the body is taken away naturally, not left to fill the blood with decaying matter ready to make aching backs, aching limbs and aching heads. Because DoOd'a Kidney Pills cure the kidneys, they cure Beckache, Rheumatism, Lumbago, Neura'gia, Sciatica and Gout. Thousands use them and thousands sing their praises. See to your kidneye ; your comfort and your life de- pend on them. Dodd's Kidney Pills are sold by Chemiste, 29 9d per box, six boxes for 131 9d ; or ant post tree on receipt of price by The Dasels Medicine Co., 23, Faeringdon ' Avenue, London, E.D. Remember the name, D-o-d-d-s. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Walton Lea Gardens Open to the Public.</p>
        <p>On Saturday,Sunday, and Monday last the public were, through the kindness of Mr 8 John Crosfiald, permitted to visit Walton Lea Gardens for the pur- pose of inspecting the beautiful collection of fizaleas and camellias. Needless to say large num- bers availed themselves of the opportunity, and the kindness of Mrs Crosfield in throwing the grounds open was much appreciated. The flowers in their several varieties, were in full bloom, and excited the unstinted admiration of the visitors. The hot- houses were well stocked with dainty blooms, and the greenery also was in splendid condition. The appearance of the gardens reflects the greatest credit upon Mr Kipp:a, the head gardener, and his assistants.</p>
        <p>Local Spiritualism: The Lyceum held its first open session on Sunday afternoon, when a fair numher of parents and friends attended to witness the proceedings. In the evening the service partook of a memorial character in connection with the death of Miss Smith, who had been planned for that day. Miss Burton, of Bury, occupied the platform, and after" reference to the departure of Miss Smith, pleaded very earnestly for more interest in the important work of the Lyceum, in connection with • which movement she acts as district visitor. The choir sang very effectively "Vital Spark," and Miss Bur- ton followed with clairvoyance. On Monday even- ing Miss Richardson, of Stockport gave good clairvoyance and psychometry. Mr. John Dun and the Extra Stamp on _ Cheques. .</p>
        <p>- • At a largely-attended meeting of the members of the London Chamber of Commerce and others held on Monday, a resolution was passed that the new stamp duty will be productive of much vexation to traders, and will disturb business arrangements. It was also resolved to petition the Chancellor of the Exchequer on the subject, and that if necessary a deputation should wait upon him. Mr J Dun (Parr's Bank) stated that there was no doubt that a 2d stamp on cheques would cause a very serious diminution in the number of cheques used. Thera would, in fact, be a practical annihilation of a mass of instruments whereby the use of legal tender was economized in this country, and less money would he in the hands of bankers. (Hear, hear.) The effect of the new impost would unquestionably be seen in the next balance-sheets of the banks. Money would be taken off the market and held in people's possession. It would prevent new accounts from being opened and cause many old accounts to be closed. There was no doubt it would be a measure in restraint of trade, and therefore it deserveu to be met with meat eneigetic opposition. (Cheers.)</p>
