      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>lettero to the Ebitor.</head>
        <p>lettero to the Ebitor.</p>
        <p>The State Church and War.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l think it may be said with truth that Chris- tianity stands alone among the religious of the world as thy only one that completely and emphatically forbids strife and violence among men, and seeks to draw them together in the bonds of mutual love and righteousness. And, therefore, when one sees professedly Christian men practically ignoring every principle of their divine faith by making war upon one another with exterminating fury then seems to be no alternative but to go outside of Christianity altogether to dtsaover some reason for their conduct. Ano, unfortunately,we have at least one poweriul reason at hand. There is a venerable and much loved Christian Church in Qur midst which does not hesitate to set its official seal to the awful crime of war. This is, no doubs. a serious and damaging charge to make against a Christian Church, but the Courch herself shall prove its truth.</p>
        <p>In her XXX VII Article of Religion the Sate Church of England [eye it down that " It is lawful for Christian men. at the commandme lit of the magistrate, to wear weapons, and serve in the wars." And this doctrine has to be sworn to by every beneficed minister of that church. Clerical utterances arc not always remarkable for clearness, but in this instance there is certainly no ambiguity and no evasiveness whatever. And your readers will observe that the doctrine is very far reach- ing sad comprehensive in its scope. Even men of the world whose standsrd of right and wrong is net always as high as it might be will admit that all wars are not alike, that same ar, just and cornmendAble, and others unjust and to be avoided and condemned. But the church allows no discrimination. Wars of defence and of aggression, and punitive expeditions for destroying the lives and property of the heathen in the interest, or supposed interest. ef trade and Christianity, are all covered with her dictum. In short, no matter what the character or purpose of a war may be, the fiat of the magistrate is all that is required to make it a lawful e ccupation for Christian men! I need harely say that no principle more opposed to the letter, spirit, and design of the Gospel of Christ than this one is, was ever formulated by Christian men. In her XXXVII " Article of religion "from which I have already quoted, the church says, "We elves roc to nor Princes the ministering either of God's word, or of sacraments," but in a matter of infinitely greater importance—the shedding of human I blood, she seems to have no ochre's in subordinating the teaching of her Lord and Master to the will of Cresar. As her conduct in this moor important matter is one of the results of the alliance between State and Church I will now offer a few remarks on that antichrist:an compact. It is well known that the rulers of this Christian country devote a large portion of their time to preparing for and carrying on hostilities in some part of the world or other. Indeed it may be truly said that war, either actual er in prospect, bulks largely in almost every sessien of Perdanaent. And as Christianity, or, to speak more correctly, what generally passes under that sacred name,ia popular our rulers very naturally desire to have 'heir nellirary operations embellished with r• ligion And their desire is accomplished in this way. Too &amp;sate has selected one of the larger religious sects and endowed it with certain privileees and emoluments, leaving the other sects to look after themselves as best they may. For the eta us and pecuni ire benefits thus conferred upon her the Sect or Church is duly grateful, and under- ' takes to do the National Chaplainicg, to pray for and bless the eoldiers when they go f(rth to kill !snow creatures whom they have never seen, but who are supposed to be their enemiee ; and to seer thanks- gieine for vict ;ries won, and for the safe return of the remnant who have escaped with their lives from the battlefield. And to this unhallowed compact much of the war spirit that has prevailed in the rast, and ie pre- veiling now may fairly be attributed. When the Church fawns upon her marten and tells them that their wars are just and righteous, these master, are gratified and encouraged to persevere in the work of death and destruction. And surely it would be vain to expect the masses of the people to les wiser or more righteous than their rulers and spiritual guides. When they roe Par- liament and the Church combined in regarding war as a Christian duty, we can hardly expect them to do otherwise. _</p>
        <p>It is a matter for great regret that a Chris- tian Church, grey with ag3, and possessing a noble liturgy, shou'd ally herself with earthly Poten- tates in legalising the awful crime of war. Love and peace with all men are the two central and essential principles of the Gospel, and tile Church which, fcr sel- fiih or political purposes, tampers with them or sets them aside attoeether, is guilty of serious disloyalty to her Lord and Dilater. Religious forms and ceremonies may, perhaps, be useful in attracting attention, but they are not,and never cm be,Christianitg. The secret of that holy religion lies in the Lord's formula, " If ye love Me k-rp My Commandments " And ene of the most precious of these cam nandments is " Love your enemies,and do good to them that hate you." Of course, if the Church were to resilc from her compact with the State and refuse to obey the will of Cwsar in regard to war she might expect to he deprived of hrr status an 4TTICIUMents, and to suffer persecution as well. But if filled with the spirit which animated the Apostles and other early followers of .the Lord how trifling would these things appear when compared with th t glorious privelige of witnepsing for Run Whose mission on earth was to girt:lly God and promote peace and goodwill among men. Yours truly, April 1902.</p>
        <p>lONA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>BILE BAN CURE NEAR WAR-</head>
        <p>BILE BAN CURE NEAR WAR-</p>
        <p>RINGTON.</p>
        <p>Agony of Piles Ended.</p>
        <p>From Warrington to Wigan is not a great di +fence, and teatimotry from so near at hand is therefore of more value than any drawn from wider range. In this instance it is a message of hope to those suffering from that painful and inconvenient ailment piles, for Chas. IForde's Bile Beans for Biliousness are constantly being preyed a wonderful and unfailing remedy. One of the latest cures thus affected is that of Mrs. eepey, of 12, Ingram-street, Woodhouse-lane, Wigan, who is now 73, I aid ha e endured the agony of tide complaint for halt her lifetime. So gratified is she by her cure that see has given to a" Wigan Examiner reporter the follow- ing details :—" For over 40 years 1 have been a victim to piles, which brought ou eenpral debility, leaving me as weak as a kittee. Often I have been overceme by a languid and out-ot-sorts feeling. The piles pagued me inwardly. I car.% describe the excrucia- ting torture . I suffered. I thought at times I should be unable to bear it any longer. Doctors attended me oe and off for many years, but neither their medicine nor ointment gave me much relief. I felt my case wss incurable. One day I read lhow Bile Beans bad wrought a complete cure in a case very like my own, so I persuaded my daughter to get me a bee. Upon its arrival I went in for a courss,and I must say I felt some little relief within a short time of the first dose. Thus encouraged, I went on taking the Beans regularly, with no result that I gradually gained is strength, and got rid altogether of that dull, languid feeling. Eventually the pains ceased to trouble me,and In the end entirely disappeared. I also felt considerably better in general health. Indeed, I seemed to be growing young again. For my cure of piles, and the tact that lam now feeling better than ever I have done for 50 years, Bile Beans are alone responsible. 1 I, shall always be glad to give them a good word wheuever I bear of anyone suffering as I have dose." Bile Beans are an unequalled remedy for piles,and are also invaluable for constipation,debility, heedache, indi- gestion, liver, and kidney disorders, loss of ambition, daz ness, nervousness, palpitation, female aidnents, pimples, face sores, and all bluo.l impuritiee. Obtain- able ,from all chemists, or poet free from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., 119 and 120, London Wall, London, 8.C., by sending price, ls la i or 23 91 per box (2a 9d box contains three times Is 10). Bile Beans are sold only in sealed boxes ; never loose.</p>
