      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISIKA rEs</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISIKA rEs</p>
        <p>SMART SENTENCE OW A,NEOLIOENT FATHER. -SHOCKING</p>
        <p>_ CONDUCT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Poliae Court on Wednesday, before Aldermen button (in the chair) and Hutchinson, James McCarthy, 511, Kuutsford-road, was charged with being drunk and disorderly In Brook-place, Latchford, on Tuesday evening ' - and also with neglecting his children.— , P.O Hardy said he was in Brook-place in company with Inspector Hackett, of the N.b.P.0.0., when he saw the prisoner, who was drunk, He spoke to him about providing food for his wife and he used very , bad language, and said he would work when he liked.— In stating the facts with regard to the second charge Mr Browne. who prosecuted on behalf the N 8 P 0 0, said the prisoner had five children. Since a week last Saturday the man had not done a day's work, and had done nothing but drink, When Inspector Hackett visited the house, in conse- quence of the complaints of the neighbours, he found there was no food in the house except a piece of dry crust. Mrs Spencer, a neighbour of the prisoner, would tell the Bench that three months ago the prisoner turned all the children out of the house, flooded the place with water and put all the bedding and the children's clothing into it.— Inspector Hackett stated that had it not been for the kind ness of the neighbours the children would have been starving Be examined all the children, and found the three older ones fairly well nourished and clean, but poorly clothed. The baby was suffering from sore eyes. The other child was very emaciated,through want of nourishment. The prisoner earned 22s a week when he worked. The prisoner came into the house during the evening. Be was drunk, and admitted that he had been drinking every day, and had brought no food into the house since the previous Wednesday. He said s "It you want to take me take me now. I will not be forced to work by any one."— Annie Spencer stated that she had provided food for the children several times. The prisoner did nothing but drink, and had been neglecting his wito and children for about three months. He did not provide any clothing for the cnil- dren.—Witness gave evidence in regard to the incident of three months ago, meetioned by Mr Browue.—Martna Malley stated that on Saturday evening prisoner threatened to throw a lamp at his wde.—Prisoner said the neighbours had joined to make bothe: between him and his wire. He had been teetotal until last week, and had never ill-used his children. He tried to do the best he contd.—Superinten- dent Monaghan said prisoner had been before the Court twice previously. In 1893 he was summoned for assaulting his wife, when a separation order was granted, and t, afterwards brought up for arrears of payment.—P was fined 106 and costs for being drunk and disorderly, and sent to prison for two months tor the neglect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>- --- FRlDAY—Before the Deputy-Mayor (in the chair), Councillors Smethurst and John Roberts, Messrs. Hepterd and Thornton. ILLTREATING A HORSE.—Jotteph Taylor, 13, Irlam-street, was summoned for illtreatlng a horse on April Bth, by cans. log it to no travelled while in an unfit state—lnspector Print. RS P 0 A., said he was on duty in Sankey-street, and saw the defendant and another man leading the horse It was horribly lame on the off fore leg—Defendant said he bought the horse from some gleades the previous day for £2 and was going to sell it when seen by the Inspector. He had since sold it to Mr Toole,the horse slaughter. Defendant was fined in the oofitS. ASSAULTS.—Mary Bllen Burke,l6,lfeaton-squ3re,w6s fined 10a for assaulting Catherine Parker, 14, Heaton-equare on April 12th.—Willians White, 58, Hale-street was fined Ls for assaulting Blizaheth Taylor, 66. Longford-street, WIFE DESERTION,—Thoma• Morris Williams, Town Hill. was summoned for deserting his wife Ellen Williams. 16, West-street —Complainant stated that for some years she and her husband lived unhappily together and eventually a mutual separation was aareed to, defendant to pay tier 7s per week. He paid her the money for a short time and then fell into arrears. She was obliged to go into the Workhouse, but sub- sequently came out, and saw the defendant with a view to making arrangements with him.—He stayed with her for one night and t hen deserted her and had never lived with her since.—Defendant said it was all through his wife's drunken habits that the home was broken up. She deserted him. The magistrates made a separation order with an allow- lance of 7s per week. MONDAY.—Defere Mr F Monks (in the chair), Alderman Robertaoouncillor Smethurst, Messrs Hayward,Thornton, Geddes and S Wright. MORE DISORDERLY CONDUCT.—Margaret Hearty, 11, Victoria-street, and Mary Grady, 9, Naylor-street were summoned for disorderly conduct in Fennel-streat on April 13, h.—P 6. Evans said the women were chat/tinging each other to fight and were making us• of bad language. —A fine of 5s was inflicted.—Daniel Crooks, 63, Watkins-etreet. William Connor, 7, Eire:mod-street, William Well, S. Phillip- street, Thomas Cartwright, 18, Melville-street, and Richard Rickards, 12, Norman-street, young men, were summoned for disorderly conduct in Orford Avenue on April 13th (Sunday) —P.O Clough said the defendants were kick- ing a ball made of rags and were shouting and jostling against respectable peaple.—Defendanta were °nab fined be and costs (115.)—William Warburton 26 Lytligoes-lane, George 11111,7 Chester-street and William Birchall,27 Birehall street, were summoned for a similar offence In Marsh House- lane, and, on the evidences et P-C Clough, were each fined bs and costs.—John Rewlands, 376 thinwick-road, John Gre -n, 3 Antrobus-street, Joseph Caldwell, 152 Winwick-road, Maggie Mahoney, 367 Winwlak-road and Martha Men Milner, 351 Winwick Road were eurnmoned for disorderly conduct In Winwick-road on April 14th,—P 0 Lewis stated that defendants were dancing and shouting and pulling each other about and a mouth organ was teeing played by some- one.—Defendante were each dried 65.—0 n the evidence of P C's Phillips and Hewitt, William Durbabbin, 6 Parr- street, and Thomas Moffatt, 45 Lord Nelson-street, were fined 2s 6d for playing football in Parr-street on April 13th. -Adam Brown, 12 Cambria-al/see, and Harry Forster, 4 Heath-lane, who were seen by P 0 Cooper kicking a paper ball about Queen-street and shouting on April 16th, were each lined 61.—William Cook and John Cook, 201 Winwick-road, were, on the evidence of P 0 Lewis, fined 2s 6d each for playing football In Longford-street on April 13th.</p>
        <p>AN OusTuNenoN.—Hugh McDermott. 22, Rnutsford•road, Was summoned for causing an •bstru tient In that thorough- fare on April 16th, by allowing a show board to project over the footpath—P.O. Reddington said the board, which was fixed in front of the shop for the purpose of displaying fish which was for sale, projected over the footpath to the extent of ten inches—Evidence having been given by P.S. Bell and Superinten lent Monaghan, defendant. who said the board did not project to the extent mentioned. was fined fis A SltklOall or THE VERT LOWEST CLASll—Echrard Hickey, of no fixed abode, who was described as a type of the habitual beggar of the very lowest class, Was .enb to goal for a month for begging in Froghall-lane on Sunday after- noon TUESDAY.—Batore Alderman Shaw Green (In the chair), Alderman Hutchinson and Dr Fox. A DESTI.RTER.—AIeaIudra Watson was charged with being a deserter from the King's Own Scottish Borderer e stotioned at Victoria Barracks, Belast —F.O Turner said he saw the prisoner in Dallam-lane with a bundle under his arm. On examining It he found it to contain a military shirt. Prisoner at first denied being a deserter but subsequently admitted that he had deserted from the regiment mentioned. Prisoner was remanded to Strangeways to await an escort, and the °mutable was recommended for the Willa( reward.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Socrety.—Annual Report. From the third annual report ot the Warrington Society, it appears that the Old Academy itself is rapidly obtaining widespread recognition. A hundred and twenty-eight names have been inscribed in the visitors' book during the year, and amongst them are the names of a number of mem- bers of the Historic Society of Lancashire and Cheshire and of the 80ld-stet Wesley Guild, whilst the Academy has taken a prominent place in various recent publications dealing with the town and has been the subject of an interesting " appre- ciation "from the pen of the Rev William Blitzebys of Sheffield, lately issued as a special supplement to " The New Kingdom" The society's collec- tion of local literature and other relics is steadily increasing, and the efforts of its members, many of whom are interested in photography, are adding a new attraction to its ancient rooms in the shape of a collection of the prettiest products of their cameras. Amongst the more notable additions to its treasures we may mention a copy of one of the old Warrington Classics (tho gift of Mr J Paul Rylands) ; and fine engravings of John Macgowan, the Bridge.street baker, who is said to have been the minister at Hill Cliffe, and is widely known as the author of "Dialogues of Devils " and 'The Shaver "; and of Thomas Pentmat, who was a fre- quent visitor to Warrington, and whose '' Tour in Scotland," and the second edition of whose great work on "British Zoology" were printed here by William Byres. The Polyglot, and Philatelic Societies have made their home once more at the Academy and the former has, indeed, shown vigorous signs of life, and carried on its meet- ings all the year. Not less than three book clubs are now running in connection with the Academy, and the formation of a photographic society is contemplated. Mr John Booth has resigned his position as caretaker, and the execu- tive have appointed Mrs James Baird as his suc- cessor. They have thought it wise to include the cottage which adjoins the main building in their tenancy, in order that they may have the whole of the Academy under their immediate control. The new arrangement has considerably added to the rooms at their disposal. The attention of local societies of a literary, artistic, or scientific charac- acter is called to the accommodation which is offered, and all Warrinvonians who are interested in any phase of intellectual life are invited to identify themselves with ths society, and assist it to increasingly fulfil the lofty aims with which is started.</p>
