      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <p>THE CORPORATION TRAMWAYS. Board of Trade Inspection. The long-deferred and much-discussed inspec- tion of the Corporation tramways by the Board of Trade took place on Thursday last week, when Colonel Von Donop and Mr Trotter, electrical engineer, visited Warrington for that purpose.' One o'clock was the hour fixed for the inspection but it was an hour later ere the inspectors put in an appearance, they having been engaged in the forenoon inspecting a line of tramway near Prescot. The representatives of the Board of Trade were met by members of the Corporation, Maior Cardew of Messrs Preece and Cardew (electrical engineers to the Corporation,) representatives of the contracting firms,and officials of the Corporation. The inspection was also atten- ded by officials of the London and North- Western Railway Company and the Post Office, , Two handsome cars were in waiting at the corner of Winmarleigh-street, and on the ' arrival of the inspectors the cars quickly filled. The first portion of the route to be traversed was from Sankey-street to Sankey Bridges. The signal having been given for the start, the tramway bell sounded its warning note, and the first car contain- ing the inspectors and other important personages moved slowly on its journey. The second car followed immediately after, and in a short space of time both ears were to be seen successfully mounting the incline of Bank Quay Bridge. A good rate of speed was obtained and the Sankey Bridges end of the system was reached without incident. Colonel Donop, who was distinguishable by his Inverness coat, descended the car and made minute inspection of the terminus, even to the extent of measuring the rails. The return ourney to Warrington had hardly been commenced when a horse attached to a lurry became frightened at tha noise occasioned by the running of the trolley wheel on the overhead wire. The driver placed his coat over the horse's head,but this only tended to increase the animal's restiveness at the new method of locomotion. A favourable oppor- tunity was taken by the drivers of the cars to get past and the horse was left prancing behind with the driver holding manfully to its head. The inhabitants—more especially the females—showed their interest in the inspection by witnessing the cars from the doorsteps of their dwelling-houses or from bedroom windows. The Post Office was reached in a few minutes and without delay the journey to the Latchford terminus was entered upon. No difficulty was experienced in negotiating Warrington Bridge and had it not been for the impetuosity of two dogs, the journey to Latchford would have been withoutfincid- ent. As it was, the two representatives of the canine race had a narrow escape.of being run over, whilst engaged in an encounter between themselves on the tram lines. They were soon made aware of their danger and they escaped with nothing more serious than a scare. A brief stay was made at the Latchford terminus and then the last homeward journey was commenced. All were congratulating themselves on the success of the inspection when an untoward incident occurred at Warrington Bridge. At this point there is a network of over head wires branching off in various direction The first car was ascending the incline on the Latchford side of the bridge when the trolley wheel which traverses the overhead wire caught one of the forks connecting the wires. The result was that the momentum of the car wrenched the trolley pole from its socket, and snapped the wire convey- ing the current to the motor of the car. A loud noise accompanied the snapping of the wire, and when a blue flame was seen to pass along the over- head wires with lightning rapidity, the onlookers were filled with alarm. To add to the confusion on the car, th 3 pole caught several of the occupants in its fall. Some of the passengers on both cars beat a hasty retreat, and a police officer in the excite- ment of the moment rushed into a cyclist, and a . horse bolted with its load and driver. The acci- dent had the effect of severely testing the strength of the overhead equipment and it is satisfactory to learn that it stood the test admirably. With the connecting wire broken, it was impossible to pro- pel the car and another car was speedily brought from the tramshed. The disabled car was fastened to it by means of chains and the journey resumed, A stoppage was made near Bridge Foot and the passengers alighted and transferred their patron- age to the car recently brought from the shed. The journey's end was reached without further inci- dent. Later in the afternoon, the Inspectors proceeded to the carshed and made an inspection of the premises: We understand that Colonel Donop un- ofaciately intimated his approdal of the tramway system and will, no doubt, report favourably to the Board of Trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Opening of the Latchford and Sankey Bridges Routes.</head>
        <p>Opening of the Latchford and Sankey Bridges Routes.</p>
        <p>Successful Start.</p>
        <p>Warrington people can now congratulate them- selves upon the successful inauguration of the electric tramway system in the borough. That the boon which has been conferred upon the inhabitants by the establishment of a frequent and regular service of splendidly equipped cars between Latch- ford and Sankey Bridges is already appreciated, has been amply demonstrated during the week when thousands of townspeople have patronized the cars, and exoressed the warmest praise of the new and efficient means of locomotion. At 7.40 on Monday morning the first public run from Itylands-street to Latchford took place, but, owing to the delay in the delivery of three motors, it was not until Wednesday morning that the service from Rylands-street to Sankey Bridges commenced A number of interested spectators assembled to wit- ness the start of the first car from Rylands-street on Monday morning, some having come with the object of indulging in a ride on the car. Amongst those who put in an appearance were Alderman Monks (chairman of the Electricity Committee, and deputy chairman of the Tramways Committee), ,Councillors Bennett and John Evans, Mr Grimedale (the manager of the tramways), and Mr Haddock, the gas engineer. Tee em- ployes who had charge of the car were Mr ,ilealey, inspector of the Latchford route; Messrs Carter and Littlewood, drivers; and Messrs Cook and ,Chetter. guards. The car was completely filled with passengers, the number on the outward journey being 61, and on the return journey 70 The run was accomplished without the slightest hitch, the time occupied being only .26 minutes including the short stoppage at Latchford. Many spectators assembled at various points on the route and the progress of the car was watched with considerable interest. A second car !eft Rylands- street ten minutes later, but there were only nine passengers. After the stoppage, however,there was Large increase, and the return journey was made with 30 passengers. During the day the cars were most extensively patronized, especially in the evening when the working classes were .at liberty. From six to about ten o'clock the cars were filled with passengers on every journey, and lively scenes were witnessed at the Rylands-street terminus. As each car drew up there was a wild rush to obtain seats, and it re- quired all the efforts of a couple of policemen and Mr Grimsdale to prevent any mishap to passengers who were entering. Notwithstanding all the rush it is grntifying to be able to report that no accident occurred. The number of passengers carried throughout the day was 5,87.5, which is equivalent to a monetary return of £24 9s 7d, which, of course, is considered highly satisfactory, On Tuesday the number of passengers was 4,43s,bringing in a sum of .£lB 9s 7d. The service to Sankey Bridges was opened on Wednesday morning. The first car an at 7 45, andl the second at eight o'clock, As the public were not generally.aware that the trams were to run there were only a few passengers. An Objectionable Passenger. Smoking Inside a Car. At the County Borough Police Court, on Tuesday, before Alderman Shaw Green and other magistrates, , Benjamin Campion, Lifford-street, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street, and Knots- ford-road. on Monday evening.—He pleaded guilty,— Joseph Healey, tramway Inspector on the Latchtord route, stated that the defendant and five others got on the 6.45 car from Latchford. Defendant was smoking a cigarette, and witness asked him to put it our. He refused and said he had paid his fare and had a right Ca smoke. lie made use of very obscene language. Witness gave information to P.C. Phillips, who requested defeodant to leave the car but he refused to do so. There were a number of ladies on the car at the time. On arrival at Itylands-strect defendant refused to leave the car, and it took two policemen and vritneis to get him out,—P.C. Phillips corroborated and said the defendant was drunk. —The Chairman said the passengers must be protected, and d.t-ndant would be fiaed 103 and costs, or in idefault 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>EXAMINER. FRIDAY APRIL 25, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. FRIDAY APRIL 25, 1902</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp;NEWTON. WARRINGTON BOARD OF EARLESTOWN DISTRICT SCHOOL. GUARDIANS. There will be an interesting ceremony in the Dr Patterson Elected Chairman. above school on Saturday afternoon, when a bust of the late Queen will be unveiled by I.3rd Newton. --- There will aho be a concert by the scholars, and we understand, Dr liandyeide, the school correipon- dent will be the recipient of some word of favour. WILFUL DAMAGE TO A GOLTSORNE PUSLIC-HOUSE</p>
        <p>On Thursday at Newton natty Sessions, Edward Wood, of oolborne was charged with doing wilful damage to a vestibule in the Queen Anne Beer- house, Golborne. William Henry Wilkinson said that because he would not serve Wood with drink on the sth just, he smashed the glass in the door. It cost 253 to repair it. He was fined 2s 6d and costs, and ordered to pay the damage. ALLEGED THEFT FROM AN EARLEsTowN HOTEL. At the same Sessions, John Boswell, a gipsy, and a well-known character, was charged with stealing a tablecloth and two towels from the Rim's dead Hotel, Earlestown, Mrs Louis Foster, wife of the manager of tie hotel, said the articles were placed in the washhouse at the rear of the hotel on the 15th inst. She did not miss them until they were brought to her by the police. He was remanded to Warrington. DAFFODIL BAZAAR. AT EARLESTOWN. The daffodil bazaar in aid of the Church Army Mission, of St. John's,Earlestown, was re-opened on Friday afternoon, when Mrs W Gamble,of Rainhill, was the officiating lady. There was again a good attendance. Mrs Gamble was supported upon the platform by Mrs Gillingwood, Mrs Wilson, Nurse Irwin (Church Army), and the Revs T R Matthews and J C Matthews. On •:aturday the bazaar was opened by the Ray. T R Matthews. Towards even- ing there was a crowded attendance, and the stalls began to wear a deserted appearance. The result of the three days' sale was about £l5O, at which much satisfaction was expressed.</p>
        <p>Sli ASH OF A PLATZ GLASS WINDOW AT EA.LDerowN. On Wednesday just about noon a large plate glass window in the tailoring e-tablishment of Messrs !Bra,!ley's, Queen-street, Earlestown, was broken into many pieces. Two men, of the labouring class, were coming up the street in an intoxicated con- dition, and oblivious to all about them, They were arm in arm and one was apparently assisting the other, but when opposite Messrs Bradley's shop, they somehow or other stumbled, and in trying to maintain their balance they finally sat down in the window amongst the mcdels and clothing. The glass was exceptionally thick, and it is marvellous that the men escaped injury from the falling pieces. Mr Hackett, ths manager,tackled the men, but they were too inebriated for any sense to be obtained from them. The damage is covered by insurance.</p>
        <p>TENT AISSTON AT EMILESTO-WN. The first visit of the Evangelization Society's Tent takes place next week, when the Evangelist will be Mr R McLean better known as an author than a preacher. The Society has been in existence for forty years and employs 250 Evangelists. They belong to all grades of Society, most of them being gentlemen who give their services free. The in- come from meetings amounts to about three thousand pounds while the expenditure is about eleven thousand. Since the beginning the Society has wrought with the clergy and ministers of all Churches. In winter the work is earried on in churches, chapels, mission halls &amp;c and in summer in over fifty tents. In the mission to be begun on May 3rd, Mr McLean has the co-operai,ion of the local ministers of all the churches who have sym- pathy with this great work. Goon TEMPLARY. ' The Hope of Earlestown Lodge meeting was held in the Ca-operative Hall. Wargrave-road, on Mon- day evening, Bro Cutler, C T., presiding. Bro Woods, L.D., read a circular from the District Sec- retary regarding the District which was refered to a committee consisting of Bros Woods, Clarke, Newman, and Hatton, Sisters Woods and Hatton. The Lodge was opened then for the public. Thanks- givings were off 3red for the success and blessing which has attended the nrder during its jubilee year. Bro Cutler, CT., in his opening remarks stated that the object of the meeting was to render thanks to God for the blessing win which Ile had blessed the efforts of the Order, not only in Eng- land where there was an increase of over 11.000 but all over the world, lodges having been formed in Hungary, and Belgium. After the singing of the ode " Hark, the temperance bells are ringing," prayer was offered by the chaplain, Sister Hatton. Addresses explanatory of the work of their depart- ment were given by Bro Woods Deputy Grand Chief Templar, Bro Clarke, Electoral superintend- ent, Bra Newman; Superintendent of Juvenile , Temple, and Bro Williamson, Past Chief Templar, solos being given by Bro G Clarke, Sister Hill who sang " Deane"; recitation, 'The water mill," Miss B Smith ; reading, "Outside and inside," Bro Webster. Bro Newman presented the greetings of Refuge Lodge, Harrington, Cumberland.</p>
        <p>ST- PETER'S Bazitia, BwrON-LE•WILLOWS This bazaar was re-opened on Friday by Mrs Airey, of Sandfield Hall. Mr W El [Hadley pre- sided. The takings that day amounted to £l2B The third day's proceedings were opened by Mr W Holland G Owen, of Rainhill. The chair was occu- pied by Mr G Airey, who was supported by Mr and Mrs W Holland Owen, the Vicar, and others. Mrs Holland Owen was presented with a magnificent bouquet of primroses, arranged with artistic taste, and Mr Holland Owen was presented with a buttonhole consisting of similar flowers The Vicar said he had a very pleasant little func- tion to perform that afternoon. It was the anni- versary of Mr and Mrs W II Hindley's wedding lday 25 years ago, so that they who were connected [ with the bazaar felt that they would like to make them a little gift on the occasion of their silver wedding. (Applause.) Twenty-five years was a long period in the lifetime of anyone and he was sure that everyone who knew Mr and Mrs Hindley would agree with him that the happiness of their married life must make that period look very short indeed to them. They were not old yet. Mr Hindley had not yet finisher! his criclaeting career—(bear, hear)—and Mrs Hindley, they all knew, had put forth great energy in connection with the bazaar. They asked their acceptance of a picture He handedl it to them with the best wishes and the hope that they might live another 25 years, when they would have something else to giva them. (Applause.)— Mr Hindley.who was genuinely surprised,expressed Mrs Hindley's and his own heartfelt thanks for their great kindness. He could scarcely express his thanks. They had lived 25 years of happiness, and he hoped it bad been the lot or would be the lot of all those present. He was sore the gift would be treasured and banded down with pride as a me- mento of a day thrice auspicious, being Primrose Day, the silver werldinF, day, and bazaar day. (Ap- plause.) He thanked all subscribers for their very kind present.—The picture was a well executed oil painting by Mr T Gee, of Newton.--The receipts up •to Saturday evening amounted to .£526 19s.</p>
        <p>The annual mesting of this Board was held on Wed- nesday morning. There were present :—Mrs Clay, Rev Father Wilson, Rev F R Matthews, Dr Patterson, Dr Murray, Messrs J Billing°, T Ritchie, T 11 Ashcroft, Wm. Hickman; S Davies, J Payton, J Robinson, J Clarke, A J )Iley, A Brooks, R Forshaw, William Houghton, J W Knowles, E L Bent, J Ball, G Pilling, T Halton, !Slather with J C Sutton (clerk), S Sutton (assistant clerk), F Gate (master), and A Wright (superintendent Padgate schools).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>TRH !%T. HELENS MAY DAY PROCESSION.</p>
        <p>The Inviter farriers of St. fir lens have written to the committee %vb.) are arranging tie S. Helena May D•y processiol offering to give a silver cup as a prize in coc- nection witii the turnonts and horses, and the offer has been accepted. ANOTHER DROWNtNG MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>St Selene is just now app..r,ntly suffering from an epidemic of drowning case', a third being reported on Thursday morning from Double Locks, Parr. Mrs Mary Ellen Clarke, wife of George Clarke, a labourer, of Barton-street. had staved at her sister's at the Double Locks, Parr, and left the house about twenta minutes to eight on Thursday morning to return to St Helens 3he had to go alongside the canal for a considerable distance and soon after eight a boy saw her in the water. ' Signalman Webster pulled her out, but life Was extinct. Deceased had bad some trouble, but there appears little evidence as to how she got into the water. —The inquest was held at St Helens Town Hall on Thursday night—George Clarke, the husband, said he was a day-wage man, but had been out of work. He knew of no trouble, and had no suspicion deceased would commit soicide.—Mre Mills, of Double Locks,said that the dec.asel was very much upset when she went to their house. Witness asked what was the matter, but she would not say and said she would know later on. Deceased was very restless during the night, and wanted to get up at four o'clock, five o'clock, and between six and seven. She had often threatened to commit suicide. She had been very curious for four or five weeks. Witness did not think she was at times responsible for her actions.—Seth Rigby, lock-keeper. said he saw deceased walking up and down near his house that morning. He afterwards got her body out of the wat-r.—fhe jury returned a verdict of " Suicide while insane."</p>
        <p>CHAMPAGNTS ANNI7AL STATISTICS.—The total quantity of champagne forwarded from the champagne districts for the twelve months ending 31st March last amounted to 23,205,440 potties. Stocks in cask and bottles equal 1,230,003 hectolitres, or about 160,000,375 bottles, con- siderably over five years' supply at present rate of con- sumption,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>The CLERK, before asking for nominations, remarked that that was the 34th or 35th time be bad been appointed chairman pro tern, under similar circum- stances to the present. Mr BILLINGE moved the appointment of Dr Patterson, the senior vice-chairman, as chairman of the Board for the ensuing year. He need not, he said, enlarge upon the qualities of Dr Patterson as a professional gentleman and, as they all knew, the doctor had efficiently per- formed all the formal offices except that of chairman. In the usual order of things he presumed they would be disposed to crown his labours by appointing him chair- man of the Board for the ensuing, year. Mr Houcinkox seconcied,and the motion was supported by Mr Ritchie, the Rev T K Matthews, Mr Mather, and Mr Beat, Mr Math3e remarking that they ouzht to have a man with some weight in the chair. (Laughter). Mr A JOLLEY : ThrOitl, throsh. (Laurtit?r.) Mr HICKMAN moved the appointment of Mr A J Jolley, and said he believed that men who gave their time to the work of the Union ought to have the honours which were in the power of the Board to confer, if there was any honour connected with the appointment of chairman. As they all knew, Mr Jolley had served them faithfully and well and bad devoted a great deal of time to his work. He had honoured and graced the chair,and although he believed in seeing those honours divided, he should like to see Mr J&amp;lley in the chair for another year. Mr Davies seconded. He should be sorry, he said, to stand in the way of anyone who was aspiring to the office, but he believed that when they had a good man they ought not to be in a hurry to dispense with his services. The Board had already admitted that Mr A J Jolley had made an excellent chairman, and that he was in every way qualified to fill the positisn. He was , always on the spot and tooklgreat pains in the discharge of his duties. They had a new master and matron, arid it was necessary that they should have a chairman whe was always on the spot to consult with them on details connected with the Workhouse. The two vice-chairmen resided out of town and occupied important positions which prevented them attending here regularly. He thought the Board might without interfering with any- body, agree to elect Mr A J Jolley for another year. Mr KNOWLES said that last year he proposed the elec- tion of Mr Jolley, and he believed that he did so the year previous, and if they kept proposing him year after year till he was dead, all the vain persons they had about them would never get to the chair. (Laughter). It was generally felt that th6re were many persons who desired the office. He (the speaker) was not as keen as ' many were. (Laughter). The time had, he contended, coma when Dr Patterson ought to be elected chairman, , as he had been for about five years either senior vice- chairman or junior vice-chairman. If Mr A J Jolley were appointed chairman of the Howe Committee he I would come into contact with the. new master and matron.</p>
        <p>Mr CLARKE supported Mr Jollev's nomination on the ground that it seemed impossible for Dr Patterson to attend regularly and perform the duties of Chairman. Mr A J JOLLEY remarked tkat on many occasions in the past the Board had shown confidence in him in the most flattering way, and he valued those expressions of opioi3s very highly. He should not have allowed his name to be brought forward except that he had been pressed by several members of the Board in such a way that he could net refuse without showing dis- courtesy to some gentlemen whose opinions he valued very highly. In those circumstances he had accepted the: nomination, but whatever the decision of the Board was, no element of personal feeling would inter— fere in any way with the relations now existing be- tween Dr Patterson and hineeelf, which had always been of the most cordial character. (Hear, hear.) Every member of the Board must use his own discretion in this matter. The appointment was immaterial to him. If the majority of the Board wished his services as ' chairman he was willing to give them, but he did not seek Office. Most emphatically he said that,at the same time he should like Dr Patterson or himself to be elected unanineously,and he wished the vote to be taken in the absence of both of them. Dr PATTEasON said be would like to see Mr Jolley in the chair &amp;pin, although he would certainly like to have the honour of being unanimouely elected to the position himself. Mr PILLING : Then I should order them both out if I were you. Mr Chairman. (Laughter.) Mr A J Jolley and Dr Patterson retired te an adjoin- ing room. The Rev FATHER WILSON supported the nomination of Mr Jolley, and spoke of his past experience and his evident ability over others to attend the meeting. Although one would like to see the honour go round, he thought the interests of the institution was the most im- portant consideration for the members of the Board. Mr CLARKE said that the chairman of the Nantwich Board of Guardians had just retired after 25 years service:</p>
        <p>..... The voting was then proceeded with, and it was found that eleven members were in favour el Br Patterson's appointment and ten in favour of Mr Jolley's The CLERK then left the room to announce the decision of the:Board to Dr Patterson and Mr Jolley,and on their return said it was now for Dr Patterson to address the meeting. .</p>
        <p>Dr PATTERSON said he WM in a Curious position. Mr Jolley and himself had been talking the matter over outside the room. He (the doctor) would like to accept the position of chairman of the Board and he was sure that Mr Jolley would give him all the help he could. (Hear. hear.) Mr A I JOLLEY : It is clearly understood that when I say a thing I mean it. Whatever the decision is I will abide by it, and I will give Dr Patterson all the support I possible can. (Hear, hear.) Mr BILLINGE explained that Dr Patterson had never asked him to move in this matter in any way. Mr HICKMAN: It was unnecessary to make such a statement.</p>
        <p>Dr PATTERSON then took the chair,and Mid he thanked the Board for the honour they had conferred upon him. He hoped he would be able to discharge the duties of the offica to the satisfaction of the Board. Previous Chairmen had always been helped by the members and the two vice-chairmen,and he had no doubt they would help him if a difficulty arose. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>VICE•CHAIRDIEN.</head>
        <p>THE VICE•CHAIRDIEN.</p>
        <p>Mr 3 W KNOWLES then nominated Mr Thomas Halton for the position of senior vice-chairman. Mr 3 BALL seconded, and said Mr Halton was oaa of th, oldest members of the Board.</p>
        <p>Mr T H ASHCROFT proposed Mr Brooks, the chairman of the House Committee, for the position.</p>
        <p>Mr S DAVIES seconded. Mil WILLIAM HOUGHTON, in supporting Mr Brooks' nomination, referred to that gentleman's services °Oche House Committee. Mr Halton had not been in close touch with the management of the Workhouse,and mere over, he (Mr Houghton) hoped that Mr Halton would soon be found filling a higher post on the Rural Council. The Bev Kurmin WILSON proposedMr William Hickman for the position.</p>
        <p>fhere was no seconder. On a rote beinc taken Mr Brooks WVI elected, ha re oeivinz 14 votes and Mr Halton 4.</p>
        <p>Mr liro-oks thm took up the vice-chairman's position and thanknd the Board for the confilence they had shown in hivi.</p>
        <p>_ Mr J BALL then proposed Mr J W Knowles for the position of junior vice-elisirman. Mr HOUGHTON seconded and the proposltion was unanimously agreed to. THE COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>Mr PILLING moved that the committees and represen- tatives ot the Board appointed at the last annual meeting be reappointed for the ensuing year. and that each of soeh committees he empowered to appoint a Chairman, vice-chairman and subcommittees, and to fix dates of their meetings. It was announced that the only alteration on the Ladle.? Committee was the substitution of the name of Mrs C Ja"lrson Holmes for that of Mr a Frost.</p>
        <p>_ Mr BALTON seconded. The proposition was unanimously agreed to. Mr KNowt.as asked whether the resolution which had been agreed to referred to the appointment of representa- tives of the Bard on the Poor Law Association. The CLERK replied in the affirmative and said the representatives were Mr A J Jolley and the Clerk. Mr KNOWLES suggested that the Chairman and the Clerk should represent the Board. The CHAIRAUN said he did not wish t 3 go. Mr KNOWLRS then suggested the senior vice-chairman, but Mr BROOKS said he would prefer to see Mr Jolley as the Board's representative. It was ultimately agreed that Mr Jolley and the Clerk should represent the Board. MR. OWEN'S REMUNERATION.</p>
        <p>Mr PILLING moved "that pursuant to sectirn 32 of the statute 31 and 32 Viet. Cap. 122, Mr William Owen be re-appointed to assist the Assessment Committee in the valuation of the several hereditament s (rateable) in the Union for the further period of one year at a salary or remuneration of £2OO for such year, such remuneration to cover and include all travelling and other expense out of pocket." Mr A Jotiity seconded and it was aareed to. CHANGES IN THE STAFF.</p>
        <p>The House Committee reported that the resignations of Miss Bailey (the sewing mistress) and of Thomas Bailey (hospital porter) were accepted and it was resolved that suitable testimonials be given them. It was recom- mended that tbe stone broken be offered to tbe Warring-</p>
        <p>ton Corporation at a rate of 93 per ton. The committee having received applications f or cook recommended that Miss Annie Milton be appointed to that position at a salary of £25 per annum. Mi‘s Ada Steer, of the general infirmary, Suffield, was appointed laundress, The minutes were confirmed on the proposition of Mr A Brooks LETTER FROM THE BISHOP OF LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>The Lord Bishop of Liverpool wrote :—" I heartily sanctior, the appointment of Rev William Bracecamp, vicar of St Paul's, Warrington,as chaplain at the War- rington Union Workhouse in succession to the Rev E R Tarbuck. Reis a good man who will care faithfully and sympathetically for the poor—Ever truly yours—F J Liverpool. J C Sutton, Esq." CORONATION FESTIVITIES FOR WORKHOUSE INMATES.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Local Governmont Board informing the Bo rd that on the dates appointed for a public holiday in celebration of the Coronation of their Majesties, the Board might make reasonable modifica- tion of the regulations in force with regard to disciplir.e in the Workhouse and incur any reasonable expense in celebrating the 'Coronation of their Majesties. The communication also stated th tt the Board was at liberty to make reasonable addition to the grants for out-relief during Coronation week. Mr KNOWLES, in referring to the suggested granting of additional out-relief, said it was probable local effort would meet the case.</p>
        <p>The CLERIC remarked that if the country parishes undertook to deal with their pear during the Coronation week it would not be necessary to make any additional grant from the rates, Mr BILLINOE thought that the relief committees ought to be empowered to make grants in any cases where no local provision had been made. Mr A J JOLLEY said in view of what the Clerk had said and in view of what was done at the Diamond Jubilee of her late Majesty, he thought it desirable that the Board should pass a resolution that no special in- crease should ba given to out-relief recipients. The Board ought to provide a treat for the inmates of the House and schools which was reasonable and proper,but he thought local efforts would meet any cases outside without the Board making special grants of money. He held that even if there were II) local elf )rts it was a dangerow thing to increase out-relief for a celebration of the kind proposed. EIC moved that no special grants be made.</p>
        <p>Mr KNOWLES selonded and ventured to think that no poor people outsido the Workhouse would go without a treat nu that day. Mr FORSHAW an::: the CHAIRMAN supported. The resolution was unanimously carried and the Board rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WARRINGTON HORSE PARADE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HORSE PARADE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee met on Tuesday, Sir Gilbert Greenall Batt, president, occupying the chair. Amongst those present were Messrs Peter Rylands, Wynne-Jones, Fletcher (Stretton), Pierpoint, FultNis Smith (treasurer), the secretaries (Messrs Venn and Pretty) and others. The principal bueiness was the reading of the sub-com- mittee's report on the route decided for the procession next Saturday, namely—Assernble in Pair ground behind Blue Coat School, proceed along Mr:wick-read, along Tanners'-lane, Fragile.ll-lane, Crosfield-street, Sankey-strret,Buttermarket,north side of Church-street, round lamp to south side Church-s'reet,tlaen along Mee- sey-stree, Bridge-street, florsemarket-street, Winwick- street, Ortord-lane and disperse on Fair Grornd (start- ing point). The Chief Constable,witia an efficiient staff of officers and men, will be in attendance to superintend and maintain order en route, while a large number of the Committee will be mounted on horseback to facilitate the marshalling, Stc., on the parade ground. Mr Joseph Taylor, treasurer ot the Infirmary Working- men's Committee, will have a large number of workers to assist him in the collection of funds en route for our local charities. _</p>
        <p>..... Many entries for competition in the various classes, of which there are fifteen, have already been booked and the Committee anticipate a very large and repiesentative turnout of all the trades in the town. Prizes and certifi- cates of merit will be awarded to drivers only—for clean and tidy appearance of horses, harness and vehicles —and diplomas to encourage kind treatment of the animals under their charge. The classes comprise brewers' vehicles, railway and canal carriers, builders and builders' merchants, contractors and carters, furni- ture removers, coal merchants and dealers, corporation vehicles, corn dealere,millers, mineral water manufac- turers, grocers, haters, milk dealers and miscellaneous vehicles; pair-horee turnouts and donkey turnouts, Competitors must be on the ground at 10-30 a.m., and the possession will start at 2 p.m.Non-competitors must I be in their places at 1.30 pm. The entries for competi• 1 tion must be handed—not later than 5 p.m. to-day—to Mr George Venn, 110, Bridge-street. The first section of the procession will be led off by a brass band followed by Walker's Brewery vehicles and Corporation Fire Engine. The second portion of the 'procession will be led off by Greenall, Whitley and Co.'s Fire Engine and brewery vehicles, also accompanied by a band. The Shim noes° Society, London, offer a silver meth! for the best single horse,and a silver medal and premium of £l. for the best pair of horses in the procession. The Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals have sent Diplomas for presentation to the winner of First Prize in each class for a clean record of kind treatment of the horse under his charge. The members of the committee will wear distinguish- ing rosettes, as follows :—President dark blue, Vice- presidents white, Judges red,and the Acting Committee, tri-colour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NOTES BY THE NAY AT EARLESTOWN. [BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE NAY AT EARLESTOWN. [BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>One Sunday the Rev Hugh C Wallace, of Bristol- who preached the anniversary sermons at the New, ton Congregational Church had a splendid recep- tion by reason of the very large congregation who assembled in the church. His addresses were of a mast powerful description' and the evening ser- mon which was especially directed to young men, contained many valuable lessons and precepts. On gambling the rev gentleman was very severe,but he said the churches before taking the gambling evil into notice should first Of all clear their own hands. By this he plainly meant, and in fact distinctly said so, drawings and raffles at church bazaars. To ask people to participate in raffies,was simply barefaced robbery, it was taking from people something and giving nothing in return. They could conduct Dazsars, he said, without drawings or raffles, and there was no difference between such, and boys playing pitch and toss in the streets. His remarks were favourably accepted, and generally agreed with. The people who indulge in these things know they are acting iliegally,and if it is wrong for the man in the street to gamble it is surely worse for the church ae a b ad}, to cauntenance it for their own gain.</p>
        <p>At another place of worship in the town, com- plaint was made of the paucity of the attendance Of course, the Rev Mr Wallace was the loadstone added to the worthy desire of the people to. help their neighbours, but the preacher evidently thoaght the congregation should not wander abroad. He descanted upon the fact, and his remarks have given offence to many people, Re was not averse, he said, to his congregation helping others, but he thought they should help themselves first. This is certainly not a Christian spirit, for it is certainly written " Help thy neighbours as thyself." It will be bad for the church over which the rev gentleman presides if in the course of a Sunday or two other congregations take to heart his observations,</p>
        <p>At last the Committee who have the question of the Coronation celebration in hand have made a move, and they have held a meeting this week and have formulated certain proposals, which include a procession of trade and friendly societies and school children, a treat to the latter, fireworks dis- play and a bonfire. The poor ratepayer has to pay for all this, but whether this will mean an increase in the rates or not, I cannot say, but if it does, I don't suppose anybody will grumble when it is con- sidered the object is a good one It is to be hoped the Committee will have the hearty support and co-operation of the townspeople and that the weather will be on its best behaviour.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Platelayers' and Workmen's Provident Society. The 27th annual meeting of the Cheshire Lines Platelayers' and Workmen's Provident Society,whichi consists of the workmen employed in the Engineers' Department of the Cheshire Lines Railway, was held at the Works, Battersby-lane. Warrington, onl Wednesday. The chair was taken by Mr W G Scott, M. Inst. CE, tne Chief Engineer of the C3capany, and the meeting was largely attended by the officialsl and delegates from Lancashire and Cheshire, The Secretary submitted tke annual report which stated that the number of members was 1,142, A large sum had been expended in aid of 267 sick members who had received assistance from the funds of the society, and twenty-six death claims had also been paid. The report and statement of accounts having been passed, Mr Thomas Pratt was re-elected .ecretary for the ensuing year and Messrs , George Walker and It Jones were re-elected audit- ors. Sincere regret was expressed at the retirement of Mr W G Scott, the chief engineer, who had held the position of honorary treasurer since the forma- tion of the society, and the members were unani- mous in wishing him health &amp;c, to enjoy his well earned rest. The proceedings terminated with a vote of thanks to the Chairman, officers, and oth eta</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>B " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>There is not very much football news stirring at pre- sent. Matches of importance are few and far between now that the League programme is complete, and the finalists for the Northern Union cup are having a " breather" prior to the closing struggle. The War- rington first team has been idle for some time, thl3 Com- mittee having apparently preferred to run the Works Competition rather than arrange a friendly encounter between the premier club and some other Northern League organisation. I rather think myself that it would have been better for the Warrington players had they had a friendly encounter ere they face Widnes in the South-West Lancashire final tie. Practice makes perfect, and just now the team is practising the art of the touch-judge in works matches instead of football. Some training is, h wever, being done and I confidently anticipate that the fifteen will prove themselves too good for the Widnesians on Tuesday next.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday I visited the chemical town with the object of seeing how the home team would fare against Leigh in the South West Lancashire Competition. They had to win this match to qualify for the final, and they succeeded after a lot of hard work. Leigh were not particularly energetic, but they presented a strong de- fence, and had not Tom Davies presented the home for- wards with a goal and a try by fooling about with the ball behind his own line, the result would have been a draw. Widnes did not seem to have the attack- ing power necessary to pierce the Leigh defence and though having the best of the game were distinctly lucky to win by such means. The football on both sides was exceedingly poor, and anything like a representa- tive display by Warrington on Tuesday should bring the cup and medals to the wirepullers," headquarters I without much difficulty.</p>
        <p>The team selected to represent Warrington is as follows : Haßant, full back ; Fish, Hockenhall,ificken- son and Harris threequarters ; Allen and Burgess half backs ; Boardman, Edmondson, Roberts, Morrison, J Swift, W Swift, Eden, and Forster forwards. This is the same fifteen which w on at Wigan and lost against Salford. Though the odds are in their favour there will have to be no shirking on Tuesday, especially among the forwards, who will meet an energetic eight. Once control is gained here, however, and the Warring- ton backs should have matters much their own way.</p>
        <p>I There is considerable enthusiasm in the town at the prospect of a cup being won, and Warrington's success would be a very popular finish to the season. This is the third final in which Warrington will have participated within two seasons,and it must not be forgotten that the third time pays for all. Though the Runcorn folk will pro- bably not patronise the match very largely,there is sure to be a good following of Warrington and Widnes sup- porters.</p>
        <p>The Northern League, the Yorkshire Senior Com- petition and the Lancashire Senior competition are stll endeavouring to patch up their little differences. The latest suggestion is that a strong second division of the Northern League should be formed of both Yorkshire and Lancashire clubs, and this idea appears an excel- lent one. It only remains for the Yorkshire Senior Competiton to assent to it for it to become law, as the Lancastrians are the proposers. An alteration of this kind would involve the extension of the Northern League, the members of which would then have a very full programme.</p>
        <p>A thirty-thousand crowd is expected at Rochdale to-day (Saturday)lto witness the final for the Northern Union Cup between Broughton Rangers and Salford. The former have already annexed the Northern League and East Lancashire trophies, and they are generally ex- pected to secure the third and most important of the lot. There is "many a slip," however, and Salford are such a good lot that they may create a surprise. In any case the margin between the teams should be small,and the struggle close and exciting.</p>
        <p>The result of the South-West Lancashire League table is as follows :</p>
        <p>Points P. W. L. D. For Agst. Fts Warrington lO ... 7 ... 2 ... 1 ... 74 ... 42 ... 15 Widnes Wigan lO 5 5 . 0 .61 ... 67 ... 10 Huneorn lO b 5 0 .46 ... 50 ... 10 Leigh Ht. Helens</p>
        <p>Widnes have had a punishing struggle lately in their efforts to get on level terms with Wigan in the Lanca- shire Senior Competition. Match after match was won with surprising regularity and some small scores, so that on Thursday the Chemicals had only to win at Barrow to tie with the Wiganers. This feat they were confident of accomplishing, though opinion in Warring- ton was very much to the contrary seeing the excellent form shown by the Barrovians in the first round of the Cup Competition. As events proved, the Chemicals' confidence was misplaced and Barrow won by a huge marginlof 18 points to nil Such a trouncing must have been a severe blow to the losers,who however,have wil- fully mislead themselves by imagining that their victories in recent engagements stamped them a class combina- tion The Barrow defeat gives Wigan the championship and they will doubtless obtain promotioa when the various Leagues,, of the Northern Union have been re- adjusted. One cannoc help feeling sorry for Widnes. They have been severely handicapped by the pressure of postponed games at the end of this season, and the hard work must have told on their energies. They have still the West Lancashire Cup to strive for, but I cannot imagine them ge tin the better of Warrington, though they will doubtless play their hardest to win.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Warrington Works Competi- tions there was another protest on Thursday evening, Fletcliers again being the team objected to, Crosfield's were the objectors but failed to substantiate their case. The difficulties of Fletcher's position are complicated by the dissolution of their combination team five or six months ago. Combination players are, of course, ineligible, but it seems impossible to prove that any of Fletchers men were members of the late combination team,and their right to play cannot therefore be abrog- ated. Still the fact that the team contains old Warring- ton first team players tells against their popularity as a works team and certainly givesithem a big advantage. There will be more stringent rules in future competitions.</p>
        <p>The final will be played this afternoon between Fletchers' and Naylors', and an exceedingly close struggle is anticipated. My sympathies go with the timber-workers seeing that they are new to football honours and are entirely composed of works recruits. They have a very good chance of securing the medals if they keep their heads at critical moments, for, taken all round they have shown smarter form than their opponents. It was unsteadiness which lost Crosfields' their semi-final, for numerous good . openings were simply thrown away.</p>
        <p>A special match has been arranged for " Wednesday evening on the Warrington ground between Latchford Rangers and a picked team from the Works Competi- tion clubs. The proceeds are in aid of two injured Rangers and I hope the public will support the fixture in a generous and sportsmanlike manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Once more the favourite summer pastime is beginning to occupy the attention of the cricket-loving public,and on Saturday the Warrington teams opened what is hoped will be a successful season. The first eleven entertained the well-known Sefton team at iirpley, and there was an average attendance of spectators to witness the engagement. Public inter- est in cricket at Warrington,however, does not seem as yet to have reached a very high pitch, the gate number- ing only from two to three hundred. No doubt the weather interfered with the attendance to some extent, the conditions being anything but ideal for the pursuit of cricket. A rather keen wind swept across the en- closure, and sweaters were called into requisition. Some interest centred in the appearance of Lynes, the new professional who hails from Halifax and has a k ood reputation both as a coach, bowler, and batsman. Apart from this change, the team remained very similar ,to the one which did duty last season, being constituted as follows : —Messrs J W Bibby (captain), J Callon, E A Connor, W H Podmore, B Silcock, Goodall P B Hawtin, J Briggs, E Gibson, G Hampson, and Lynes. The new profes- sional had prepared an excellent wicket, and as if to celebrate the opening of the season, some additiona bunting was displayed. The visitors won the toss and batted first. The Rev F Paterson and Mr F Parrington were opposed by Lynes and Gibson. The professional had hard luck with the first ball sent down, completely beating Paterson. The score rose slowly to eleven, when Paterson returned one to Gibson, and in the same over the bo *ler met with another success, bowling Blundell with a .fine length ball.</p>
        <p>Parrington, together with A Boswell, scored freely and at 25 E A Connor relieved Lynes. The score rose steadily to 37, when a fine return from Briggs was re- sponsible for the dismissal of Parrington.</p>
        <p>A vigorous display of hitting by Kitchener and Boa. well was witnessed and Goodall failing to meet with success Gibson again took up the attack at the town end, promptly meeting with success, bowling Boswell on his first over. Five runs later E Connor sent back Blease, whilst at the same total a break-back proved too much for Owen.</p>
        <p>With Williams as Kitchener's associate the score rose rapidly, and the introduction of W Podmore tended to improve the fast rate. The century was hoisted, but the cheers had scarcely subsided acknowledging the</p>
        <p>feat when Kitchens skied one to mid on and Goodall brought off a neat catch. Williams continued his merry career, and Lynes was called up again, and speedily got the Sefton captain leg before.</p>
        <p>Another fine piece of fielding by Briggs sent back Williams for an invaluable 38, and the innings closed for 146.</p>
        <p>, --- The excellent bowling of Gibson was the feature of the innings, his achievement being the capture of six wickets at a cost of only 33 runs His impending de- parture from Warrington will be a decided loss to the team and will be much regretted. Lynes was instru- mental in obtaining one wicket for no runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington had a little over an hour in which to save the match, but their opening essay did not augur well, for with his second ball Kitchener shot down B Silcock's off stuntp. Steady play by P B Hawtin and several fine strokes by J Callon, however, put a better facing on the game, and the aggregate had reached 19 before Callon was bowled for a useful dozen.</p>
        <p>E A Connor came next and his innings proved mo Y valuable. He played both Grewcock and Kitchen with confidence and several of his drives were ver fine. At 32 Hawtin unluckily played one hard on to his leg stick, and an encouraging cheer greeted Lynes on his taking the vacant place at the wicket. His first stroke was a fine drive off Kitchener to the bowling green boundary, but at 41 he failed to get a thorough hold of one from the Sefton professional, and was easily taken at mid off.</p>
        <p>With J W Bibby next the score rose steadily ; and the aggregate reached 70 when Mr Connor was bowled for a faultless 30, the fall of this wicket bringing the match to a termination, the result being a draw.</p>
        <p>The second eleven 'visited Sefton Park and sustained edefeat by nine runs, the scores being 73 and 64 res- pectively. A. Duckworth batted: in good form for Warrington, his innings realizing 16,and C. H. Adamson knocked up 14. For the victors A. H. Davies headed the list with 20. The Warrington men did well in dis- missing their opponents for the total of 13 and it was indeed hard lines to be beaten by the snaall majority of nine runs.</p>
        <p>The following fixtures have been arranged for the ensuing season by the Warrington first team:—' April. July. 25—Newton a s—St. Helene</p>
        <p>I—Northern h May. 17—Liverpool a 3 —Bolton ll 19—Bootle a 10—Boatle h 26 —Rarleetows a 17 —Northern a Aug. 21 —LI verpool h 2 31—Cheetham a 9 —Cheatham h</p>
        <p>16— June. 21- 7—Newtou h 23 —Leyland 14—St. Helena a 30—Leyland h 21-11ar1estown l3 Sept. 28-13olton a • 6—Jefton a</p>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>The opening run of the season in connection with the Lovely-lane Cycling Club took place on Saturday after- noon, Altrincham being visited. Although the weather was not very favourable, the attendance of members was highly creditable, sixteen taking part in the journey, seven of whom were ladies. The total number of members of the club is now upwards of thirty. Mr John J Foden is the Hon. Secretary.</p>
        <p>Ihe said " I suppose you wish me to dismiss Thomas," witness replied, " Nay, I have no authority to give you that word of command." Bramball said, 'lt I means the same."</p>
        <p>Mr Joseph Edge, the local secretary of the union, said the delegates had no authority to do more than tell Mr Bramhali what the plaintiff had done. Mr Acton : Supposing you won't have him as a member of your socie.ty, and supposing be gets work anywhere and the union men come out, how is he to get a living ? Witness : There are places in the town for men like him to work at.</p>
        <p>It was shown that the Society had resolved that in the event of the members corning out they were to re- ceive trade privileges.</p>
        <p>The Judge said the question to be decided was whether the decision to get Thomas dismissed was coma to in furtherance of some object for the benefit of the workers, or whether the guiding motive was to punish him for working at Naylor's. People had no right to constitute themselves a tribunal for the punishment of individuals, but it was perfectly lawful for people to combine to promote trade objects, even though in so doing individuals were injured. Mr Shee argued that if it was found that the motive was punishment the question would arise whether what was done was an act of the Society, or whether the Executive had exceeded their authority. Be submitted that the Society had done nothing illegal. Prima facie every member of the Union was retitled to just as much liberty as a non-unionist. The first complaint was not from the Society or tne branch, but the men them- selves. They had found. out that Thomas had received strike pay and then turned his back on the Union. The jury must concede that the men themselves had a griev- ance, because if they countenanced such conduct as Thomas's it would be useless and extravagant expense in the future to support non-unionists in the event of a strike. It was impossible to say in this case that the object aimed at was not the general interests of the Society, which the men were entitled to proteot. The Judge, in summing up, said if the jury thought that, although the defendants were annoyed with Thomas because of what he bad done, their real motive was to advance their trade interests and not vindic- tively to punish the man, the verdict should be for the defendants. It,on the other hand, the jury thought the guiding motive was to punish Thomas because he had donelsomething of which the defendants disapproved,the verdict ought to be for the plaintiff. The questions his Lordship would leave to the jury were those :—(1) Did Ward and Fothergill give Brarnhall and Bissell to under- stand that if Thomas were not discharged they would call the men out ? (2) Did that bring about Thomas's discharge ? (3) In doing so did they carry out the in- tentions of the Warrington branch of the Society? (4) Was the guiding motive of this action to promote the interests of the Society and its members or to punish Thomas for having taken piecework elsewhere ? The jury, without leaving the box, found for the plaintiff on all points, and gave him £lOO damages. On the application of Mr Acton the Judge granted an injunctiol against the defendants on terms to be agreed upon between counsel. Th,</p>
        <p>lat very old-eatatdisnea engineering business Messrs Beyer, Peacock, and Co., Ltd., of Manchester, whose locometives are known all over the world, and hold a record for stability and workmanship, is on the eve of undergoing a large and important conversion. This concern has always sustained the British reputation for railway engines, and has held the palm in competition with American and other firms. Large orders for South Africa will follow the announcement of peace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>ACTION AGAINST A TRADE UNION.</head>
        <p>ACTION AGAINST A TRADE UNION.</p>
        <p>Alleged Boycotting of a Warrington</p>
        <p>Joiner.</p>
        <p>At the Manchester Spring Assizes on' Saturday before Mr Justice Wills and a jury, the case of Thomas v. the Amalgamated Society of Carpenters and Joiners was beard. This was an action to recover damages from a trade union for unlawfully procuring the oisinissal of a workman and for conspiring to prevent employers from giving him work. The plaintiff—represented by Mr Acton and Mr Hulton—is a joiner at Warrington, and the defendants were the Amalgamated Society of Car- penters and Joiners. Mr Shee, EX., and Mr Wilkinson were counsel for the society. The case for the plaintiff, Sydney Joseph Thomas, was that certain members of the Joiners' Union at Warring- ton bad brought about his dismissal by a threat that if he were not sent away the other workmen would be withdrawn. He applied to be admitted a member of the union and had beers refused, on the ground, it was said, that he had on one occasion accepied strike pay from the union, been enabled by the union to find work elsewhere, and bad on his return to Warrington taken work in a " black," shop. Mr Acton, in stating the facts of the case, used the word " boycotting" as des- criptive of the society 's ccnduct towards the plaintiff. The reason why the plaintiff went to the " black " shop, said Mr Acton, was because he had a wife and five chilaren to keep, and he could not find employment anywheee else. Subiequintly, counsel added, Thomas got employ- ment with a Mr Davenport, who was at the time building the Technical School in Warrington. The local branch of the union thereupon Sent a depot +Hon, consisting of Mesirs Ward and Fothergill, to Mr Bramhall (Mr Daven- port's manager), and Mr Bissell, the foreman joiner, the last-named being a member of the society. This was early in December last year. They told Mr Bramball that Thomas was not a member of the 'neon, and Mr Bram- hall said he never asked him if he was when he engaged him, adding that at the same time he took one or two other men who be knew were not members. " Yes,' said the delegates, "but they never worked at Naylor'. Thomas did. If Thomas stays with you all onr men shall come out." Mr Bramhall said, " There is my own son Billy : he is not a member." The delegates replied that his case was different also ; he bad never worked at Naylor'. The result of the threat was that as the firm desired to avoid a strike, Thomas was givea two boars' notice to leave his work. After this Thomas went hither and thither in search of employment, but failed to get it, and he was breught to the verge of star- vation. Eventually he got s situation at Crosfiele's, in Warringten, and then his case was brought under the notice of the geineral secretary of the society by the Warrington branch. By this time these proceedings had been started, and the secretary in his reply to the branch said Thomae must not be interfered with, also stating that anything the branch might do might be brought up in evidence against the society. Counsel contended that if it was right that Thomas should not be interfered with while he was working for Crosfield's it was right that this should have been the case when be ! was working for Mr Davenport. If the object with ! which the society acted was to injure Thomas, and did injure him, he submitted that it was an illegal act. Their only object was to boycott Thomas and to prevent him from earning a living, as a punishment for having worked in a " back' shop. Thomas had repented for having done so,he had asked for admission to the society and had been refused, and after that this action was taken against him. Thomas did not want to make money out of this case. He estimated his loss by being , out of work through the action of the society at £5O. In , addition to this he asked for reasonable damages and for 'an injunction restraining the union from interfering with him.</p>
        <p>Mr J Bramhall. the manager for Mr Davenport in the building of the Warrington Technical School, said that after Thomas had been at work for about four weeks two men named Ward and ntbergill came to the works. Witeas said, " Whatever is the matter ?" They replied " We have come to see you with regard to Sid Thomas." " What is the matter win Sid Thomas ?" he asked, and the reply was that they (the union) had assisted him at the time of the strike, had seat him to Buxton to work, that whilst at Buxton be paid 23 6d as proposition money to join the union, and that when he returned to Warrington be went to work at Naylor's, which was considered a " black " shop. " I gave them " said witness "to understand I bad nothing to do with the question whether a man was in the union or out of the union. If a man pleases me and I want one I take him on, and when we don't want one I stop him, Irrespective of trade society matters." Witness men- tioned the case of his own son Billy, who was a non- society man and had also been sent to Buxton. " Oh," they said, "he has done 'dating contrary to trade interests." Witness turned to his foreman. Biesoll, who is a society man, and said, " This is a bit thick,"or something to that effect ; " what are we going to do ? " Ward raid, " Well, we will come again to-morrow." They had been betore to see Bissell. Bissell and witness " considered the matter over and came to the conclusion to stop Thomas." " fhey gave us to understand, " the witness added. "at the fret meeting that unlese Thomas was moved if the job they would withdraw tneir men from the Jb. Neat day I told them what we bad done, and of course they went away smiling ; they were satisfied." Be had no reason of any kind for discharging 'llamas but the compulsion of these delegatee. He must either aiticharge him or ' injure his masters by baying a strike. In answer to Mr She- the witness said he had heard from Bissell before the delegates came that there was going to be bother if Thomas was kept on the j -43, and that the other men were determined to go out. Be certainly thought Thomas had not acted fairly towards the union in doing what be did af:er taking their pay, but that would not have prevented bth, employment. giving Thomas Mr J Biesell, the foreman joiner, corroborated Mr Bramhall as to the interview with the delegates. He said tbe delegates at the first interview gave him to un- derstand that they the ether society me were going to withdraw him and nif he did not get rid of Thomas. The plaintiff said be was disiniesed by Mr Bramhall on the 6th December. Be did not work again until February, and then only for a f.,rtnight, at Crosfield's, a friend having intervened in his behalf. A few weeks ago, Mr Bramhall, who was now working on his own account, had taken him on. For the defence Mr Ward, one of the delegates, was , called. He denied that they threatened to withdraw the men. They told Mr Bramball certain facts, and when '</p>
      </div>
