        <p>THE CORONATION. --- Meeting of Schools' Representatives in Warrington. On Friday evening a meeting of the repreeentatives ef Ithe:various Sunday schooltilwas held at the Town Hall for the purpose of making arranpernents for the procession of scholars on Friday, June 27th.--The Deputy-Mayor presided over a large attendance. The Mayor -was unable to be present as he was detained in London. The Camels/or, in explaining the objects of the meet- ing, said it had not been thought desirable by the Coronation Committee to keep alive Walking Day as Walking Day this year, seeing that it occurred on practically the same date as the Coronation, and it , would be advisable on financial and public grounds to merge tbe two occasions in the one clay, Juno 27th. The ' Coronation Committee proposed to make the grant as was done in Jubilee year, of sixpence per head to each school, in order that there might not be the necessity for soliciting publie subscriptions in addition. Already a large number of subscriptions bad been given on the understanding that they would be utilized, so far as the grant was concerned, for the purpose of defraying the expense of such a treat as the managers of the schools might consider desirable. On the proposition of the REcTon,seconded by the Rev Father WILSON, it was decided that the procession take place on Friday June 27th, and that the usual Walking - Day be merged into:that occasion. Some discussion took place asto the grant of sixpence per head, several representatives of the schools express- ing the opinion that it would be insufficient without soliciting additions! subeeriptions. Ultimately it was decided that the grant be accepted it being left to the discretion of thci managers of the schools as to whether subscriptions should be solicited in addition. The following sub-committee was appointed to ar- range the details :—The Rector, Rev E C E Catleton,and Mr Stansfield (representing the church of England schools), the Revs T Bishop, John Yonge, and C Finch (representing the Nonconformist schoels), the Revs Fathers Whittle, Wilson, and Ryan (representing the Roman Catholic Schools), Messrs Craik and Fairclough (marshals), and the Chief Constable. The CHAIRMAN said it was hoped that the funds would permit the purchase of medals,.but that matter would be reported upon later. The meeting then concluded.</p>
        <p>Lancashire County Asylums Bill. On Monday Lord Stanniore's Committee concluded the consideration of the Lancashire County Lunatic Asylums Bill. The question raised in the Bill is the , substitution of user for ratable value as a basis of 'contribution. The County Council assented to the iproposal, which was put forward on behalf of Man- cheater and twelve other county !boroughs, end opposed by Liverpool, Bolton, and Wigan. Mr Littler, IC C, in stating the case for the obj?ctoi a, said he relied strongly upon the contention of an absolute Parliamentary bargain. The rule Parlia- ment had adopted, so far as he knew, was that they would never, under any circumstances, set aside a bargain unless they could put the parties into the same position they occupied before,and they would not even do that unless it were shown that the bargain had been made under a misapprehension. Mr Fitzgerald, for the promoters, remarked that thirteen out of eighteen boroughs, after ten years' trial,found the result of the agreement to be unjust lin the working and, therefore, asked Parliament to revise it. The Chairman, after consultation with his colleagues, announced that they were unani- mously of opinion that the preamble was proved. ete. COMMERCIAL FA/LCEll4.—According to 6' Keinre Mercantile Gazette," the number of New Bank- ruptcy Proceedings in England and Wales gazetted during the week ending April 19th, was 89. The number in the corresponding week of last year was 93, shoeing a decrease of 4, being a net decrease in 1902, to date, of 52. FLORTLINE 4—FO • THE TEETH AND Brocs.xe.- -Thoroughy cleanses the teeth from all parasites or impurities, burden the gums, previ.nte tartar, stops decay, and gives to the teeth a peculiar pearly whiteness and a delightful fragrance to the breath. Price qs. fid. for the liquid, or Is. per Jar for the Floriline ",Powder." of all Chemists and Perfumers.— Adult VALUABLE DISCOVERY FOR THE ACLIR,—Ii your hair Is I turning grey or white or fallin